{
  "raw": {
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Operating-Engineer_R4937": {
      "title": "Operating Engineer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Operating Engineering 1 Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Full On Campus Presence Union: Excluded Job Description: Summary: The Operating Engineer, under the direction of the Assistant Chief and Lead Operating Engineers, works interdependently to resolve facility issues and customer concerns. Provides support in the operation, maintenance, and repair activities associated with the University's utility systems. Supports the resolution of after-hours and weekend requests. May be required to handle the responsibilities of other Operating Engineers or Assistant Operating Engineers in their absence. Coordinates and reviews the work of others, and trains and mentors new staff members and others as appropriate. Essential Functions: Participates in the operation, maintenance (preventative and predictive), trouble shooting, and repair of campus thermal systems (including but not limited to steam, chilled water, and heating water), related plant equipment, water treatment systems (including but not limited to natatorium systems, boiler systems, and chiller systems)   as well as maintenance and repair of domestic water & sewer campus distribution systems, and campus gas utility distribution systems to assure the fitness for use and reliability of our buildings, systems and equipment. Trains Assistant Operating Engineers and FMT as required with regards to the above systems. Operates and maintains systems to meet department, reliability, and energy efficiency objectives along with regulatory compliance requirements. Maintains records pertaining to the inspection, operation, maintenance and repair of campus utilities systems, and related plant equipment to build a database from which historical data can be used to optimize scheduling and repair of critical components of the heating and cooling systems. Makes recommendations to improve the efficiency and reliability of campus managed utility systems. Responsible for the proper storage and usage of tools and equipment used in the repair operations to ensure the availability of an adequate operating inventory of tools and supplies. Performs inspections of campus utilities systems, and related plant equipment tests to ensure that boiler and chiller water chemistry is within acceptable limits and maintains proper chemical water treatment through the knowledge and application of approved training provided by the chemical distributor. Supports in the resolution of afterhours and weekend request received though the customer response center line (2Fix). Completes approved training and performance requirements to meet department goals as well as regulatory requirements. Maintains an IDP (Individual Development Plan).  Ensures all items are documented in the LMS (Learning Management System). Competencies: Championing Customer Needs. Building and Supporting Teams. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 40 hours per week. Evening and weekend work is required. This position will be required to work multiple shifts. Current shift schedules are (7AM to 3PM, 3PM to 11PM, 11PM to 7AM). This is a relief position, 24/7 coverage. The incumbent will be required to work a second consecutive shift should their relief fail to arrive for the next shift. The incumbent is required to work unscheduled shifts as needed. The position will perform job duties in confined spaces and be exposed to extreme temperatures. This is a physically demanding position which requires strenuous physical exertion such as frequent or constant lifting, pushing, and/or pulling of heavy objects to perform essential functions, with or without reasonable accommodation. Salary Range: $37.25 - $41.00 per hour. Required Education and Experience: High school diploma or equivalent. 1 - 3 years of relevant experience working in: 1.) A plant having high pressure steam boilers not to exceed four hundred (400) horsepower or a combination of high- pressure boilers and heating boilers or refrigeration equipment, not to exceed a total of four hundred (400) horsepower; 2.) A heating plant having low pressure steam boilers with a pump return and refrigeration equipment, with no limitation on total capacity; or 3.) A heating plant having hot water boilers and refrigeration equipment, with no limitation on total capacity. Preferred Education and Experience: Facilities Management Certificate. LEED Green Associate Certification. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: 1.) Required Education & Licenses  H.S. Diploma or equivalent. 3rd Class DC Steam Engineer’s license (non-restricted) by the date of hire. Must possess a current and valid United States driver's license at the time of hire and be able to obtain and maintain University driving privileges. 2.) Central Plant Operations Strong working knowledge of and the ability to perform moderately complex technical tasks to support the operation, maintenance, and repair of assigned utility systems and related equipment. Ability to understand blueprints and moderately complex technical manuals. Strong working knowledge of the tools, equipment, regulations, and procedures relevant to site-specific systems. Ability to become proficient in all site-specific systems. 3.) Math & Science Knowledge of practical math, percentages, decimals, and fractions, length, area, and volume measurement. Knowledge of and ability to understand fundamental principles related to thermodynamics and basic water chemistry. 4.) Building Automation Systems (BAS) Basic knowledge of BAS and ability to independently resolve routine problems. 5.) Decision-Making & Leadership Receives general supervision. Ability to independently resolve moderately complex technical problems and serve as a point of escalation for nonroutine problem resolution. Ability to review one’s own and others’ work to ensure completeness and accuracy, carefully prepare for meetings, and organize information or materials for others. 6.) Communication & Interpersonal Ability to express oneself orally in a clear and concise manner, while seeking and accepting feedback in a non-defensive manner, and providing constructive feedback and instruction to coworkers as appropriate. Ability to produce accurate and clear written material on topics of moderate complexity that is appropriate to the audience and free of spelling and grammatical errors. Proactive and demonstrated commitment to treating all individuals with dignity, compassion, and respect. 7.) Technology Ability to use Microsoft Outlook, Word, and PowerPoint to prepare briefings, reports, and presentations. 8.) Physical Requirements Ability to lift and carry 50lbs. Ability to climb ladders and work at heights. Ability to work in confined spaces and areas with temperature extremes. 9.) Other Requirements Must be at least eighteen (18) years of age by the date of hire. Interest in and commitment to ongoing professional development and training. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Assistant-Professor-in-Biotechnology_R4551": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Biotechnology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: College of Arts & Sciences Time Type: Full time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Biology in the College of Arts & Sciences at American University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in Biotechnology at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 1, 2027. The ideal candidate is a collaborative scientist with an internationally recognized or emerging research program that leverages biotechnology to address important questions in biology, medicine, health, agriculture, or related fields. Areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to, synthetic biology, genome engineering, computational biology, bioinformatics, systems biology, microbiology, biomedical engineering, or other biotechnology-related disciplines. Candidates with experience in technology translation, entrepreneurship, industry collaboration, or intellectual property development are encouraged to apply. Preference will be given to candidates who are able to develop partnerships with biotechnology companies, government laboratories, hospitals, nonprofit organizations or other regional institutions that create experiential learning, internship, and collaborative research opportunities for students. This individual will complement the Biology department’s strengths and establish synergies with other departments in the College of Arts & Sciences. Applicants should have demonstrated effective teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Candidates with mentorship experience, and those able to work with a variety of students are preferred. Responsibilities will consist of building or maintaining a nationally and internationally recognized research program, developing and teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, mentoring undergraduate and graduate student researchers, and participating in department, college, and university service. The successful candidate will occupy lab space in the university’s new Hall of Science, a state-of-the-art facility that houses the Biology, Environmental Science, Chemistry, and Neuroscience departments. The successful candidate will join a collaborative department with strengths spanning molecular biology, cell biology, virology, genetics, neuroscience, physiology, ecology, and evolutionary biology. They will have opportunities to develop interdisciplinary collaborations across the College of Arts & Sciences and with institutions throughout the Washington DC metropolitan area, including NIH, NSF, DoD, FDA, the Smithsonian, research hospitals, nonprofit organizations, biotechnology companies, and neighboring research universities. The annual salary range is $100,000-111,000. Applicants should have a PhD or an anticipated PhD completion by August 1, 2027. Full review of applications will begin October 16, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled, subject to on-going budgetary approval. Applications should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement (including accomplishments and future directions), teaching statement describing how prior experience and future goals will advance the department’s commitment to students, and recent teaching evaluations (when possible). Letters of recommendation will be requested from select candidates after application review begins. Please direct any questions to Dr. Naden Krogan at nkrogan@american.edu. American University is a private institution within easy reach of the many centers of government, business, research, and the arts located within the nation’s capital. For more information about American University, visit www.american.edu. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Desk-Assistant--Student-_R5086": {
      "title": "Desk Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Residence Life Training Leadership Programs Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Desk Assistant (DA) is a part-time student staff member of Housing & Residence Life and a member of the University’s Division of Student Affairs.  The Desk Assistant fulfills a vital role in the residence hall system by helping ensure the safety of the halls, assisting residents with concerns, and welcoming guests and visitors. The DA is supervised by a Residential Operations Coordinator with the support from the Assistant Director for Residential Operations and Finance and is expected to work closely with other staff members (other DAs, Desk Coordinators, Resident Assistants, Community Directors, Facilities Management staff, etc.) within the building, as well as across campus. This position staffs the residence hall front desks from 7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily. Essential Functions: 1.) Administration Turn in completed HR and payroll forms to the Residential Operations Coordinator or Assistant Director for Residential Operations and Finance.  Also, submit appropriate HR paperwork via WorkDay. Be timely and professional in all written and verbal communications with Housing & Residence Life staff and AU community members. Compile accurate and timely reports for supervisor as requested (shift logs, digital systems management). Complete and submit bi-weekly online timesheets on time. Treat information concerning staff and/or students with the utmost confidentiality. 2.) Community Facilitation and Customer Service Contribute towards the safe living environment for the residents. Answer questions from students, families, faculty, staff, prospective students, and alumni in person and over the phone. Follow proper protocol to check in and out guests of residents. Treat the concerns of all customers with attentiveness and seriousness. Maintain a working knowledge of the University’s facilities, services, and resources. Ensure that the desk is clean, all binders and logs are in appropriate locations, and all supplies are stocked. 3.) Role Modeling and Policy Compliance Monitor and enforce access to the residence hall. Be well versed in, personally abide by, and help enforce University policies including the Housing Agreement.  DAs must also abide by all regulations in federal and local jurisdictions. Confront, document, and follow through on violations of published University and Housing & Residence Life policies, regulations, and procedures in an accurate and timely manner. Abide by policies and procedures aligned in the protocol manual regarding building evacuations. Understand that all actions of a DA (including those engaged outside of the residence hall) are observed by residents, other DAs, and staff members. DAs must hold themselves to a higher standard in all actions. Follow outlined emergency response protocol at the desk. 4.) Facilities/Operations Assist in the opening, closing, and staffing of the residence hall desks at break times. Assist residents with checking in and out of their housing assignments using the StarRez housing assignment management software system. Follow lockout protocol to assist residents with regaining access to their rooms when locked out. Check out administrative keys to groups that have residence hall space reservations. Follow proper key distribution procedures to maintain accuracy and security. DAs must not create or issue keys to anyone, including themselves, without following proper documentation procedures. 5.) Communication Maintain positive relationships with fellow Desk Assistants, Desk Coordinators, Resident Assistants, Community Directors, HRL staff, and Facilities Management. Attend DA meetings to schedule desk shifts and receive additional training. Maintain ongoing communication with Residential Operations Coordinators, fellow Desk Assistants, and Housing & Residence Life office by checking phone messages and e-mail messages daily. Attend all scheduled shifts on time. Follow proper communication protocols when seeking emergency shift coverage.   Follow proper communication protocols when communicating with on-call staff and emergency personnel. 6.) Other duties as assigned The nature of the DA position does not allow for all duties to be explicitly described.  There will be times when hall staff are requested to assist in an emergency or other unforeseen circumstances.  These situations will be presented as thoroughly as possible at the time of need. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 12 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Full-time enrollment as an undergraduate or graduate student at American University (minimum 12 credits and a maximum of 17 per semester). If a Desk Assistant wishes to drop below the minimum credit hours, they must receive written permission from the Director of Residence Life to retain the DA position. Cumulative GPA at, or above, 2.50 and term GPA at, or above, 2.50 (GPAs must be maintained from the date of application until the date of separation). Must be in good conduct standing with the university. Participate in the Desk Assistant interview process. Desk Assistants are expected to work a minimum of 12 hours per week, including 3 hours on Saturdays or Sundays. Desk Assistants are required to work during one break (Thanksgiving Break, Winter Early Arrival, Spring Break). Desk Assistants are required to work during Finals and hall closing periods. All Desk Assistants must attend and fully participate in staff training in August and January prior to the start of the academic semester. All Desk Assistants are expected to work during the opening of residence halls. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: This is a 10-month position from August through May. Staff are required to be present and participate in all DA training and residence hall opening and closing dates. All dates are subject to change. Applicants are required to attend one Desk Assistant information session. This position has “nontraditional” business hours making it likely staff will work evening and early morning hours. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Resident-Assistant--Student-_R5085": {
      "title": "Resident Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Residence Life Training Leadership Programs Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Resident Assistant (RA) is an undergraduate or graduate part-time student staff member of Housing & Residence Life and a member of the University’s Division of Student Affairs. The RA fulfills a vital role in the residence hall community by connecting residents to resources, providing advice on how to navigate the collegiate experience, and creating enriching living environments. The RA reports to the Community Director (CD) and is expected to work closely with other staff members (other RAs, Desk Assistants, Living Learning Communities, Themed Communities, etc.) within the building, as well as across campus. Essential Functions: 1.) Community Engagement Serve as the leader in developing and facilitating an environment on the floor and in the Residence Hall in which students can grow and learn socially as well as academically. Plan and implement learning and engagement opportunities for residents as part of the Eagle Flight Plan, focusing on leadership, self-discovery and community responsibility. Advise and refer their residents to campus resources to aid in supporting their academic, personal, and social well-being. Complete Clawed Chats for their floor/community aimed at developing and building a rapport and relationship with the residents that live in these areas. Submit at least two (2) “Of the Month” award nomination per semester through the National Residence Hall Honorary (NRHH). 2.) Emergency Response Serve in a community-wide on-call rotation. RAs can expect to be on call [regularly, including some weekends. The RA on call must remain in their residential community and be available to respond to emergency concerns. RA on call shifts occur every night from 8 pm - 8 am , and on weekends from 8 am - 8 pm. RA on-call responsibilities include staying in the residence hall to be accessible to residents, responding to any issues that arise, and monitoring the residential environment by (1) Completing community rounds to maintain the safety and security of the building. (2) Ensuring external doors and windows are locked and secured. (3) Reporting communal facilities concerns. (4) Documenting potential policy violations. (5) Acting in the capacity of a Desk Assistant should a coverage issue arise (paid hourly) during on call hours. (6) Providing support and resources to students in need. (7) Granting students access to their rooms if locked out (“lockouts”). RAs are required to serve on call during university breaks and holidays. These include Thanksgiving Break and Spring Break. RAs may also be directed to serve on call during unexpected university closures. When serving on call, RAs are not permitted at any point to leave their assigned residential community. RAs serving on call are not permitted to take the RA on call phone off campus. RAs are expected to return the on-call phone to its designated location when no longer serving on duty. In the event of a significant residence hall or campus emergency, RAs are expected to assist, if needed.   3.) Administrative Duties Support department-wide initiatives. RA recruitment and selection information sessions, tabling, and interviews.   Other university and departmental events that may require staff assistance. Conduct regular administrative tasks on behalf of Housing & Residence Life, including the completing of Room Inventory Forms (RIFs), documenting potential policy violations, and reporting facilities concerns. Complete Health and Safety Inspections for their floor/community at least two (2) times a year. Complete accurate and detailed incident reports, Equity/Title IX(ETIX) reports and other important reports immediately using either the ETIX link or Guardian software.   4.) Opening & Closing of Residence Halls Help prepare for check-ins, checkouts, openings, and closings. RAs are expected to assist with these duties prior to and at the conclusion of the fall and spring semester.   5.) Desk Assistant Resident Assistants are required to serve 3 hours each week as a Desk Assistant in their assigned community, which is included in their compensation package. RAs are permitted to work additional hours at the front desk at the Desk Assistant hourly wage rate. Resident Assistants are required to participate in scheduled online or in-person training prior to the start of the fall and spring semesters. Resident Assistants are also expected to participate in ongoing development through in-service meetings. RAs are expected and required to be at all components of fall and spring training. No Resident Assistant is excused. Resident Assistants are required to attend weekly staff meetings. RAs are not permitted to schedule classes or other commitments during this time. RAs are also expected to participate in regular one-on-one supervisory meetings.   6.) Outside Involvement and Employment After academic commitments, the RA position will be the staff member’s priority. Housing & Residence Life, in consultation with the RA involved, reserves the right to limit time commitments that may interfere with their job performance. Outside commitments include other student leadership/organization positions, additional jobs, non-academic credit internships, etc. Resident Assistants are permitted to maintain outside commitments with prior approval from your direct supervisor. Academic student internships are limited to three (3) credits per semester. Resident Assistants must submit a written request about any additional job, internship or University leadership responsibilities detailing the anticipated time commitment to their direct supervisor within 48 business hours of receiving the offer. Prior approval is required for participating in other University Leadership positions (e.g., Orientation Coordinators/Leaders, Student Government , etc.). Due to conflicting responsibilities, RAs are not permitted to be hired as Desk Coordinators, or to be elected or appointed members of the Residence Hall Association.   Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 15 hours per week. Salary Range: $375 stipend per month. Required Education and Experience: Full-time enrollment as an undergraduate or graduate student (minimum 12 credits and a maximum of 17 per semester for undergrad & minimum 9 credits and a maximum of 12 per semester for graduate students). If a Resident Assistant wishes to drop below the minimum credit hours, they must receive written permission from the Director of Residence Life in order to retain the RA position. Complete one (1) semester of full-time undergraduate education by the start of employment. One (1) semester living on campus at American University. Cumulative GPA at or above 2.5, and term GPA at or above 2.5 (GPAs must be maintained from the date of application until the date of separation) Must not have been found responsible for violating a serious conduct policy, residence hall regulations, or University Policy (must be maintained from the date of application to the date of separation). All applicants must attend an information session by December.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: This is a 10-month live-in position from August through May. The RA responsibilities and time commitment are determined by the needs of the department. The RA role requires flexibility to support an occasionally unpredictable environment. Housing credit added to the student account to cover the cost of a single room in the assigned residence hall. Resident Assistants are not required to have a meal plan. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Experiential-Education-Assistant--Student---FWS-_R5081": {
      "title": "Experiential Education Assistant (Student) (FWS)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Experiential Education Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Experiential Education Assistant will gather information to identify students engaged in experiential learning activities and provide administrative support to experiential education projects managed by the Career Center. They will achieve this by reviewing data collected by the university, researching open-source material and communicating with staff, faculty, and students. In addition, the Assistant will provide administrative support for experiential learning management.  This position reports to and assists the Experiential Learning Specialist.  Essential Functions: 1.) Data Management Collecting data from reports and managing the data in Microsoft Excel. This includes categorizing the data based on established criteria, curating outreach, research lists, and standardizing written responses.  Resolving conflicting data points using critical thinking skills.   Researching via information sources such as social media (primarily LinkedIn, though others may also be sources) and Handshake.   Configuring and assessing Excel data sets to fit internal reporting standards.  Maintaining clean data records and easy-to-navigate documentation in our shared drive and in Microsoft Teams.  Managing incoming information and applications related to internship support and engagement. Entering data according to standards and criteria.   Creating reports with visual and numeric components.     2.) Outreach and Communications  Creating social media. Requesting data from faculty and staff across campus via email.  Managing the internships@american.edu email box, responding to standard inquiries and elevating issues as necessary.    3.) Other Duties Perform other duties as assigned. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 8 - 12 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Incoming or current AU student with an active Federal Work Study (FWS) award. Must be residing in DC, Maryland, or Virginia during the period of employment. Experience using Microsoft Excel to manage large data sets. Good communication skills. Dedication to ethical decision-making, data integrity, and confidentiality. Willingness and ability to learn new software and/or technology. Ability to communicate effectively with people of different backgrounds and positions. Ability to adhere to strict protocol/standard operating procedures.   Preferred Education and Experience:   Intermediate proficiency with Microsoft Excel, filters, tables, and charts. Familiarity with LinkedIn, Handshake, Microsoft Outlook, Word, and Teams. Familiarity with career services and various forms of experiential learning (including internships, study abroad, academic research, leadership activities, volunteering).     Additional Eligibility Qualifications:   Periodic meetings with the Program Coordinator of Experiential Learning and will be required (in-person and/or virtual).    This is a part-time, 8 - 12 hours per week student position that begins in October 2026 and concludes on or before May 7, 2027. Continuation of the position after May 2027 is possible contingent on continued availability of funding and the incumbent’s continued enrollment at American University and agreement by all parties.  These qualifications would contribute to being successful in this position. Some candidates may feel that they must have all the skills and experience before applying; we remind you that this is not the case. We will consider other relevant qualifications when evaluating your application for this position. We strongly encourage students of diverse backgrounds and experiences to apply.  Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Geospatial-Research-Lab-Assistant--Student-_R5073": {
      "title": "Geospatial Research Lab Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Digital Research & Inquiry Lab Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Student Geospatial Assistant will primarily be responsible for digitizing and georeferencing historical maps and data for use in academic research. Working with the Program Director for Geospatial Research Support in the University Library, the Student Geospatial Assistant will also help plan and run a creative mapping workshop and a community mapping event. No prior GIS experience is required; the Assistant will be trained in the core digitization skills the position uses and will gain hands-on experience with GIS software, data creation, metadata, and open-data publishing.   Essential Functions:  Georeferencing and digitizing historical maps. Extracting geographic features and building GIS data layers. Writing and publishing metadata. Assisting with the design and facilitation of a creative mapping workshop. Helping organize and run a Humanitarian OpenStreetMap (HOT) map-a-thon. Documenting digitization workflows for future use.   Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 10 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: High school diploma or its equivalent. Must be a current AU Student.  Attention to detail and the ability to work independently once trained. An interest in maps, geography, history, data, or GIS.  Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Helpful but not required: familiarity with ArcGIS or QGIS, GitHub, coding, graphic design, or cartography. No prior GIS or mapping experience is required; training is provided. Approximately 10 hours per week, scheduled flexibly around the student's class schedule.   Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/East-Campus-Washington-DC/Guest-Engagement-Coordinator_R5060-1": {
      "title": "Guest Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Business Development Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: Excluded Job Description: Summary: Th Guest Engagement Coordinator is a member of the University Conference & Guest Services (UCGS) team within the division of Auxiliary Services and Finance. The Guest Engagement Coordinator will be the first point of contact for internal and external conference clients and guests and will manage UCGS communications as the initial point of contact. This person will be responsible for maintaining the UCGS website as well as data input within for the Intern Portal within Star Rez and Event Guru. This person will oversee all Intern housing components as well as other individual paying clients during the summer and will oversee year-round housing for Interns in Clark Hall. This position is also the sub-scheduler in EMS for the UCGS event spaces and will handle systems administration for Event Guru and Star Rez for Intern business.   Essential Functions: 1.) Communication Respond to initial inquiries about university conference and events. Approve or decline space reservations of UCGS spaces in EMS for Internal clients. Work with Director and Associate Director in gathering initial marketing information to maintain UCGS internal website, Destination DC and Unique Venues websites as well as other forms of Social Media. Produce informative weekly newsletter during summer months for Summer Student Staff. Assist Associate Director with the design and creation of UCGS marketing materials both on-line and hard copies. 2.) Guest Services Interpret client needs, anticipate potential problems, and determine the best solution for having a successful event. Keep detailed data in a customer relationship management system of all event planner information, as well as prospects gathered through various marketing strategies. Work with approximately 15 Intern and AU Internal Groups as well as 400 individual paying guests in the summer and 2-3 Intern Groups and 30 individual paying guest during the academic year by taking reservations, preparing contract information, working with the finance team with on-line payment and answering questions of each client. Serves as point person for UCGS and mail services to ensure data is prepared and disseminated so that clients may receive mail services  year-round. Provide visible, on-site coverage for clients as needed. 3.) Staff Recruitment, Selection, and Training and Supervision Support the University Conference & Guest Services team with summer student staff selection processes and training programs. Supervise (12-15) student staff  year-round. Supervise (1) Summer Leadership Student from January to August. 4.) Systems Administration Primary point of contact for administration of UCGS software solutions with Event Guru and Star Rez which are used in planning and preparing for Intern reservations, payment access as well as other services that include electronic access, security, and card production systems. Oversee the training and effective utilization of relevant UCGS software systems to ensure information in all systems is accurate, up-to-date, and audit compliant. Facilitate account access, training and troubleshooting support for all UCGS users when using Event Guru and Star Rez for Intern housing.   5.) Other Duties as Assigned   Supervisory Responsibility: Supervises 12-15 students year-round. Competencies: Displaying Creativity.   Prioritizing and Organizing. Serving Customers. Supporting Coworkers. Leading and Inspiring People. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Building and Supporting Teams. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Hours are 8am - 4pm or 9am - 5pm. Hybrid work modality (on campus 3 - 4 days per week). Requires some non-traditional hours during peak operational periods. Salary Range: $27.50 - $29.50 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor’s Degree. 1 - 3 years of relevant experience. At least one year of experience in event planning, university housing and/or summer conferences required. Computer experience, particularly in database and spreadsheet applications, required.  Must be able to prioritize, balance, and complete multiple tasks with direct but minimal supervision.  Excellent customer service skills required. Requires non-traditional hours during peak operational periods.   Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree. Knowledge and experience with the following software programs: email, word processing, spreadsheet/data analysis and presentation development software applications such as Microsoft Outlook, Word, Excel, EMS, STAR REZ and Event Guru. Supervision of paraprofessional staff. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/4401-Connecticut-Campus-Washington-DC/Writer-Editor_R5056": {
      "title": "Writer/Editor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Community Communications Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Writer/Editor identifies, pitches, and crafts compelling, high-impact stories across American University’s flagship enterprise channels. Working closely with the editor and a dynamic creative team of designers, videographers, and photographers, this role drives community-centered storytelling, transforming institutional milestones, research breakthroughs, and individual voices into vibrant narratives for diverse audiences—including prospective and current students, alumni, faculty, staff, donors, and more. In this role, the Writer/Editor researches, reports, and writes engaging content for American magazine, the weekly AU Now newsletter, university web pages, marketing campaigns, and multimedia scripts. The position executes an array of storytelling formats—including in-depth narrative features, profiles, research stories, institutional news, trend pieces, and event coverage (including occasional evenings and weekends). The Writer/Editor experiments with fresh narrative formats, distinct angles, and creative storytelling techniques tailored to shifting audience interests and digital reading habits. Additionally, they deliver polished, accurate, and fact-checked copy on deadline, maintain a consistent brand voice across all media, and actively contribute to pitch meetings, long-term editorial planning, and cross-functional creative strategy to bring the AU story to life. The ideal candidate brings a demonstrated track record of reporting and writing versatile, engaging stories across print and digital platforms, supported by exceptional interviewing, research, and narrative development skills. They think boldly, embrace creative risk-taking, and pivot seamlessly between fast-turnaround assignments and long-form features. Strong project management skills, meticulous attention to nuance, and a commitment to producing clean copy under tight deadlines are essential, as is a collaborative mindset honed by partnering with visual creatives such as designers, photographers, and videographers. A background in copyediting and proofreading (The Chicago Manual of Style) or multimedia production (scriptwriting, video, or social) is a strong plus. Essential Functions: 1.) Content Reporting and Development Identify, report, and write a wide range of print and multimedia content for American University’s flagship enterprise channels, including American magazine, AU Now, university web pages, marketing campaigns, and multimedia scripts. Research story ideas, develop interview questions, cultivate on- and off-the-record sources, and produce clear, concise, and colorful copy for assignments ranging from 200-word department pieces to 3,000-word features to a holiday video script. Copyedit and proofread content as needed. 2.) Editorial Planning and Source Cultivation Collaborate closely with the editor to brainstorm story concepts, plan dynamic editorial calendars, and develop enterprise packages for the university. Identify students, faculty, staff, alumni, and community members with compelling narratives; cultivate a robust network of sources across campus and beyond; conduct background research; and track emerging trends in higher education, alumni communications, and digital media. 3.) Digital Production and CMS Support Build and publish stories within the content management system (CMS), assisting with the ongoing maintenance and digital presentation of the university’s news and magazine sites. 4.) Other Duties as Assigned Support broader university communications initiatives and perform other related editorial or creative duties as needed. Competencies: Displaying Creativity. Prioritizing and Organizing. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Developing Plans. Thinking Broadly. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Hybrid work modality (three days in the office). Salary Range: $70,000.00 - $75,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 5 - 8 years of relevant experience. At least five years of professional writing experience—preferably with a news outlet or higher education institution. Preferred Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree. 8 - 10 years of relevant experience. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Strong writing, copyediting, and proofreading skills, along with familiarity with videography, social media, and digital publishing. Self-starter with an entrepreneurial approach to their work, capable of juggling competing priorities and deadlines while thriving under pressure. Confident interviewer and savvy researcher. Excellent news judgment, strong diplomacy skills, and ease in both academic and non-academic settings. Proven ability to write compelling, accurate content in a variety of styles for diverse audiences. Collegial and flexible team player who brings empathy, a collaborative spirit, and a good sense of humor. Ability to seamlessly transition from interviewing the university president for a magazine feature to shooting tiny-mic videos with students on the quad. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/4401-Connecticut-Campus-Washington-DC/XMLNAME-1A-Plus-Podcast-Producer--Producer-I-_R4939": {
      "title": "1A Plus Podcast Producer (Producer I)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Production Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: Screen Actors Guild - American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA) This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by the Screen Actors Guild - American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA).This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by the Screen Actors Guild - American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (SAG-AFTRA). Job Description: Summary: WAMU 88.5 is a public media station owned and operated by American University. WAMU is the source for local, regional and NPR news and original programs in Washington DC. Since 1961, WAMU has been amplifying voices and sharing stories from the many neighborhoods and communities in the District of Columbia, Maryland and Virginia. The 1A Podcast/ Plus Producer is responsible for the integrated daily production and distribution of both the 1A podcast and 1A Plus products (versions of the daily program created for member stations that don’t air 1A live), ensuring timely and uninterrupted delivery to both broadcast and digital audiences. Working under the direction of the Senior Supervising Producer, this individual serves as the primary producer responsible for the daily preparation, editing and distribution of these products This includes re-packaging and re-editing content from the live version of 1A that airs each morning to ensure it a) meets the needs of the dozens of broadcast stations that air 1A Plus across multiple time zones and b) that the broadcast program is appropriately edited to meet the differing expectations of the on-demand podcast audience. The producer edits content as needed, coordinates host retracks, ensures the various products meet all timing requirements, and incorporates sponsorship messages, funding credits, and other required program elements. In addition, the Podcast/Plus producer uploads and distributes both podcast and Plus versions, ensuring all deadlines are met.    This position reports to the Senior Supervising Producer, 1A.     Essential Functions : 1.) Produces and Edits the Daily 1A Plus and 1A Podcast Products Repackages and re-edits the live 1A broadcast into products tailored for both member station and on-demand podcast audiences. Edits and assembles program content, including pacing, timing, and flow while preserving the editorial integrity of the original program. Supervises host retracks and incorporates sponsorship messages, funding credits, and other required program elements. Ensures all products meet editorial, broadcast, and technical quality standards. 2.) Performs the Technical Production, Quality Control, and Distribution of the Daily 1A Plus and Podcast Products Oversee final production workflows to ensure timely and uninterrupted delivery across broadcast and digital platforms. Performs final quality assurance and verifies all timing and formatting requirements are met. Uploads, publishes, and distributes podcast and 1A Plus products according to established production schedules and deadlines. Troubleshoots production and distribution issues to ensure reliable daily delivery. 3.) Supports the overall production of other 1A programming, with specific responsibilities for producing Rebroadcasts (shows re-aired on holidays etc.) and Evergreen Editions (the regularly updated emergency programs provided to stations in case of significant technical difficulties). 4.) Provides guidance and technical assistance to 1A staff and collaborators regarding podcast and 1A Plus production workflows and serves as a resource to support operational continuity. Competencies: Serving Customers. Displaying Creativity. Prioritizing and Organizing. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Hybrid work modality (3 - 4 days per week in office). Salary Range: $68,656.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 1 - 3 years of relevant experience. Experience in audio production, journalism, broadcast production, or a related field. High level of aptitude with audio editing and sound design, with a firm grasp of one or more editing platforms. Experience of and aptitude for working on tight daily deadlines. Strong communication skills, with the ability to offer and accept constructive feedback. Highly organized, experience setting up audio file inventories and tracking tape assets. Demonstrates an interest in mentoring other teammates, including freelancers and interns. Strong writing skills and editorial judgment. Experience using multimedia content production and other online tools to edit photos, audio, and video, (WordPress, Adobe, Final Cut Pro). Experience working with content management systems (CMS). Proven ability to work collaboratively as part of a team. Driven and determined, demonstrated creative problem-solving skills. Keen understanding of national and international current events, and excellent news and narrative judgment. Time management skills, flexibility, and ability to work effectively in a dynamic, unpredictable news environment. Committed to creating an inclusive and respectful workplace and team environment. Preferred Education and Experience: 3 - 5 years of relevant experience. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Tenley-Campus-Washington-DC/Part-Time-Career-Counselor_R4881": {
      "title": "Part-Time Career Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Washington College of Law Time Type: Part time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary:   The Part-Time Career Counselor provides individualized and group law student advising, identifies and develops resources for students and alumni, and designs and presents educational programs and events. Each Career Counselor also assists with other counseling and programming initiatives of the Washington College of Law (WCL) Office of Career & Professional Development (OCPD), including serving as the liaison between the office and assigned WCL programs, institutes, student groups, and other internal and external organizations and associations.  The Career Counselor may also develop additional specialties as needed, such as sector- or practice area-specific expertise. The Career Counselor will work closely with a team that includes counselors, recruiting professionals, and administrative employees.       Essential Functions:   1.) Career Advising Advise law students on all aspects of career planning and job search methods, including self-assessment, resumes, cover letters, interviewing and networking to aid students in their job searches and career development.   2.) Programming Develop and facilitate job search skill development workshops and other educational programs and initiatives. Identify content areas and coordinate seminars, panel discussions, and other resources that meet the needs of students interested in a broad range of employment sectors and practice areas.      3.) Industry Analysis and Engagement Analyze legal trends and develop employer networks based on student and office needs. Use employer contacts to gather information about job and hiring trends and inquire about internships and employment possibilities. Use knowledge gained to educate other members of the OCPD staff and incorporate knowledge during individual counseling sessions with students. Work with OCPD’s recruiting team members to conduct outreach and create interactive opportunities between OCPD and employers.      4.) Professional Development Participate in professional organizations related to career counseling and legal employment. Review publications to keep abreast of job market trends including the National Law Journal/Legal Times and in-house counsel publications.    5.) Other Tasks Assist the Senior Director and other team members with counseling, programming, outreach, and engagement as directed. Work closely with fellow counselors to ensure consistent messaging under the guidance of the Senior Director. Complete projects as tasked to assist with the overall goals of the office.     Supervisory Responsibility: May supervise student and/or administrative staff.   Competencies: Championing Customer Needs.  Developing Plans. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions.    Evaluating and Implementing Ideas.  Building and Supporting Teams.    Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 27 hours per week. Some evening and weekend hours are required.   Salary Range: $35.00 - $40.00 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Juris Doctor or equivalent. 2 - 4 years of relevant experience, w hich may include legal experience, recruiting experience and/or counseling experience.   Preferred Education and Experience:  Juris Doctor.    3 - 5 years of relevant experience.     Travel Required: Periodic travel for professional conferences is required.    Additional Eligibility Qualifications: We strongly encourage applicants to submit a resume and cover letter describing their interest in and qualifications for the position. Must possess excellent interpersonal, organizational and oral, and written communication skills. Knowledge of job search, career/ employment services, recruiting, interviewing and staffing principles and practices.  Must have a broad understanding of career counseling techniques with specific knowledge of legal market trends and job search strategies.  Ability to assess individual needs, motivators and differences in abilities and career interests, including those in the public and private sectors. Must be comfortable working in a fast-paced environment and marketing the Washington College of Law to employers. Other Duties:   Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time, with or without notice. Benefits Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
