    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/4401-Connecticut-Campus-Washington-DC/Video-Producer_R5026": {
      "title": "Video Producer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Community Communications Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Video Producer position is part of the University Communication and Marketing team. The position is responsible for every aspect of video production from pre-production through to post-production, including concepting, planning, producing, shooting, interviewing, editing, mixing, color correcting, and incorporating music, graphics, and captions. The Video Producer is expected to understand and apply the best practices for video storytelling and audience engagement. The position must manage multiple assignments, moving from cutting brief clips, making quick edits, and working on longer-term projects as required.   Responsibilities include developing content ideas; coordinating, directing, and filming a variety of footage including event coverage, interviews, b-roll, and promotional content; as well as managing all aspects of post-production including editing and mixing for clarity, emotional impact, and audience retention. The position is expected to output final cuts that are optimized for different platforms, ensuring correct aspect ratios, length, and attention‑grabbing edits; create multiple edits and formats from one core piece of footage; and leverage AI-assisted creative tools. In addition, the Video Producer will have strong time management and project organization skills to ensure videos are properly tagged, titled, and stored.   The Video Producer must have exceptional videography and Adobe Premiere editing skills along with strong script writing and storyboarding capabilities. The person will remain poised and professional even with quick turnarounds and changing priorities and consistently demonstrate excellent communication skills and time management abilities. The Video Producer will collaborate with the internal UCM team to shape multimedia messaging that aligns with brand goals and maintains consistency. They will operate as part of the AU community and work with campus colleagues to create video content that supports their project goals.   Essential Functions: 1.) Video Production and Project Management Produce short and long form video, web and social media video assets, and other multimedia content. Plan, produce, edit, and deliver video and digital content that supports AU’s strategic priorities, including but not limited to enrollment, retention, university reputation, academic profile, research, scholarship, and community engagement through strong storytelling and visual composition. 2.) Story Collaborator Work together with UCM colleagues to develop stories and video content that supports university priorities, enhances university reputation, contributes to core functions such as enrollment and retention, and delivers key messages.    3.) Campus Partner Engagement Develop and maintain relationships with internal stakeholders to leverage video asset sharing across the university. Coordinate regular meetings and interactions with videographers and other campus digital creators. Create a shared video editorial calendar. Competencies: Displaying Creativity. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Acting Strategically. Supporting Coworkers. Developing Plans. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Prioritizing and Organizing. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Potential evening/weekend overtime requirements for university events. Salary Range: $35.71 - $40.00 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience.   Preferred Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree. 4 - 6 years of relevant experience. Videography experience with Canon DSLR and RED digital camera, preferred.   Travel Required: Candidate will be expected to have ability to travel locally to different project and/or event locations.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Please share a digital portfolio that demonstrates experience writing, shooting, producing, and editing engaging video content in a variety of styles for diverse audiences. Video editing software experience and expertise – Adobe Premiere, CapCut (or similar). Excellent research, organization, and time management skills. Proven communication and collaboration skills. The capacity to work both independently and collaboratively. The ability to be flexible and work efficiently without compromising quality or accuracy.  Demonstrated knowledge of professional editorial practices and processes. An understanding of analytics and content performance metrics.   Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Lead-Operating-Engineer_R4247": {
      "title": "Lead Operating Engineer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Operating Engineering 2 Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Full On Campus Presence Union: Excluded Job Description: Summary: The Lead Operating Engineer serves as Team Lead and oversees the work of Operating Engineers and other trade staff within the Energy Management unit performing the inspection, efficient operation, maintenance, and repair of campus utility systems and related plant equipment. Under the direction of the Assistant Chief Engineer, this position works independently with leeway to resolve facility issues and customer concerns, while ensuring adherence to quality standards and providing exceptional customer service. Essential Functions: Serves as Team Lead. Schedules work assignments, sets priorities, and directs the work of Operating Engineers and Assistant Operating Engineers. Identifies staff training needs and ensures training is obtained. Participates in the operation, maintenance (preventative and predictive), troubleshooting, and repair of campus thermal systems (including but not limited to steam, chilled water, and heating water), related plant equipment, water treatment systems (including but not limited to natatorium systems, boiler systems, and chiller systems)   as well as maintenance and repair of domestic water & sewer campus distribution systems, and campus gas utility distribution systems to assure the fitness for use and reliability of our buildings, systems and equipment. Operates and maintains systems to meet department, reliability, and energy efficiency objectives along with regulatory compliance requirements. Maintains records pertaining to the inspection, operation, maintenance and repair of campus thermal systems, campus domestic water & sewer distribution systems, campus gas distribution systems, water treatment systems, and related plant equipment to build a database from which historical data can be used to maximize scheduling and repair of critical components of the heating and cooling systems. Makes recommendations to improve the efficiency and reliability of campus managed utility systems. Responsible for the proper storage and usage of tools and equipment used in the repair operations conducted by the position to have tools available and in proper operating condition when needed. Performs facilities commissioning activities (e.g., reviewing of new and upgraded mechanical equipment or systems) to ensure that operational, maintenance, repair, and energy efficiency impacts on the Central Plant Operations and Energy and Engineering units are minimized. Completes training and performance requirements to meet department goals as well as regulatory requirements. Maintains an IDP (Individual Development Plan).  Ensures all items are documented in the LMS (Learning Management System). Supports the resolution of afterhours and weekend requests received though the customer response center line (2Fix). Prepares accident/incident reports and documentation for recommended corrective actions related to the Staff HR Policy manual. Competencies: Championing Customer Needs. Building and Supporting Teams. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 40 hours per week. Evening and weekend work is required. This position will be required to work multiple shifts. Current shift schedules are (7AM to 3PM, 3PM to 11PM, 11PM to 7AM). This is a relief position, 24/7 coverage. The incumbent will be required to work a second consecutive shift should their relief fail to arrive for the next shift. The incumbent is required to work unscheduled shifts as needed. The position will perform job duties in confined spaces and be exposed to extreme temperatures. This is a physically demanding position which requires strenuous physical exertion such as frequent or constant lifting, pushing, and/or pulling of heavy objects to perform essential functions, with or without reasonable accommodation. Salary Range: $43.00 - $48.00 per hour. Required Education and Experience: High school diploma or equivalent. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience. At least two (2) years’ experience working in a plant having high pressure steam boilers not to exceed four hundred (400) horsepower or a combination of high pressure boilers and heating boilers or refrigeration equipment, not to exceed a total of four hundred (400) horsepower; a heating plant having low pressure steam boilers with a pump return and refrigeration equipment, with no limitation on total capacity; or a heating plant having hot water boilers and refrigeration equipment, with no limitation on total capacity. At least one (1) year of experience as an assistant engineer in charge of a 3rd Class plant or as a Class 2 steam engineer. Preferred Education and Experience: Facilities Management Certificate. LEED Green Associate. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: 1.) Required Education and Licenses  H.S. Diploma or equivalent. 3rd Class DC Steam Engineer’s license (non-restricted) by the date of hire. Must possess a current and valid United States driver's license at the time of hire and be able to obtain and maintain University driving privileges. 2.) Central Plant Operations Thorough knowledge of and the ability to perform complex technical tasks to support the operation, maintenance, and repair of site-specific utility systems. Ability to understand blueprints, general engineering drawings, and complex technical manuals. Thorough knowledge of the tools, equipment, regulations, and procedures relevant to site-specific systems. Proficiency in all site-specific systems. 3.) Math and Science Knowledge of arithmetic (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, percentages, fractions, roots, exponents, and the ability to perform math operations based on word problems), decimals, length, area, and volume measurement. An understanding of the fundamental principles of thermodynamics, chemistry, and energy efficiency relevant to the work performed. 4.) Building Automation Systems (BAS) Working knowledge of BAS and ability to independently resolve moderately complex problems. 5.) Decision-Making and Leadership Receives limited supervision. Ability to independently resolve complex technical problems and serve as a point of escalation for moderately complex problem resolution. Ability to schedule work assignments, set priorities, train, and direct the work of others, and ensure adherence to quality standards and timelines. Ability to motivate others by personal example and by direct coaching to increase their abilities to demonstrate respectful and compassionate interaction. 6.) Communication and Interpersonal Ability to express oneself orally in a clear and concise manner, seek and deliver constructive feedback, and adapt communication style to the task and audience. Ability to produce clear, technically accurate, and accessible written materials on complex topics. Demonstrated commitment to self-awareness and desire to continuously improve one's ability to interact with others in a respectful and compassionate way. 7.) Technology Ability to use Microsoft Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint to prepare briefings, reports, and presentations. 8.) Physical Requirements Ability to lift and carry 50lbs. Ability to climb ladders and work at heights. Ability to work in confined spaces and areas with temperature extremes. 9.) Other Requirements Must be at least eighteen (18) years of age by the date of hire. Interest in and commitment to ongoing professional development and training. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Spring-Valley-Campus-Washington-DC/Sponsored-Award-Specialist_R5019": {
      "title": "Sponsored Award Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: SO000016 Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 02 (On Campus 2 days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: This position provides the full range of technical and consultative services to faculty and administrators seeking and receiving external funding for research, education, service, training, and other programs from government agencies, corporations, private foundations, and other external funders for a designated portfolio of primarily standard and moderately complex proposals and awards. The incumbent in this position will (1) provide technical, budgetary and consultative service to faculty and administrators seeking external funds from government agencies, corporations and private foundations to further the research initiatives of the organization; (2) represent the university before external sponsoring agencies (federal, state and private) in negotiating and administering contractual and grant instruments within defined authority thresholds; and (3) assist administrators and faculty in administering external funding in accordance with relevant sponsor policy, federal regulations, and AU policies.   The Sponsored Award Specialist reports directly to the Director for Sponsored Awards, in ORSA and under the functional guidance of a designated Sr. Manager Grants & Contracts to ensure that both pre-award and post-award actions meet sponsor requirements, regulatory standards, and institutional policies. The Sponsored Award Specialist works alongside a team of other Specialists and serves as a primary institutional administrator for an assigned portfolio of work. Specialists provide essential assistance to faculty and administrators in securing and administering grants and contracts, and is responsible for ensuring that programs adhere to the terms and conditions specified in externally funded agreements.   Essential Functions: 1.) Central Research Administration and Lifecycle Management Serves as the primary grants administrator for assigned department(s) or unit(s), under the guidance of the Director of Sponsored Awards, working closely with the Senior Manager of Grants & Contracts, and  in accordance with institutional and sponsor policies ensuring they meet agency and university guidelines, published timetables, and deadlines with Authorized Organizational Representative (AOR) approval.   Pre-Award activities include: Coordinating proposal documents with unit administrators including budgets, budget justifications, effort (labor) allocations, etc. to ensure completeness and institutional readiness for AOR authorization Confirming proposal budgets are accurate, allowable, and aligned with sponsor and institutional requirements Compiling centralized reports for federal and sponsoring agencies, Coordinating subrecipient documentation, and ensuring required representations, certifications, and compliance elements are complete Ensuring timely and accurate electronic submission of proposals via Cayuse 424, Grants.gov, Research.gov, ASSIST, (or other submission platforms), Serving as a central institutional resource to unit-based pre-award teams regarding administrative requirements, timelines, and system routing   Post-award activities include: Administering standard and moderately complex grants and contracts to ensure adherence to terms and conditions. Establishing and maintaining accounts in the appropriate AU system of record, Processing amendments and modifications, and prior approval actions in accordance with sponsor requirements Coordinating the approval and timely submission of interim and final reports, sponsor renewals, progress reports, just-in-time documentation, no-cost extensions, and re-budgeting requests following sponsor award reporting terms and deliverables for reporting, Retrieving actions through the University systems (Cayuse SP, Cayuse 424, Workday, Business Intelligence), ensuring alignment with grant budgets and timelines. when necessary. 2.) Outreach, Training & Professional Development Participates in research administration training programs for the University community. This includes attending quarterly grant roundtable meetings and department meetings and participating in the University Research Administrator Certification Training program. Remains up-to-date with all University, federal and funder financial and budgetary guidelines related to externally funded grants and contracts. 3.) Additional Duties Additional responsibilities as assigned or requested by the Director of Sponsored Awards, Associate Director of Spnosred Awards, or Vice Provost for Research & Innovation.      Competencies: Serving Customers. Prioritizing and Organizing. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Supporting Coworkers. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $70,000.00 - $75,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree. 2 - 4 years of relevant experience in sponsored research administration, grants and budget management, contract administration, or a closely related field, with responsibility for supporting pre-award and/or post-award activities in a higher education or research environment. Demonstrated experience supporting the preparation, review, or administration of grant and contract proposals and/or awards, including familiarity with institutional and sponsor requirements. Knowledge of governing regulations related to grants and contracts, including OMB Uniform Guidance (UG) and the Federal Acquisition Regulations (FAR). Evidence of excellent demonstrated time management skills including the ability to meet strict deadlines while maintaining high quality work and attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple tasks, including follow-through and tracking. Strong technical skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with faculty, administrators, and central offices. Demonstrated ability to work independently with minimal supervision and collaboratively within a team and across campus units.   Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience in sponsored research administration, grants and budget management, contract administration, or a closely related field, with responsibility for supporting pre-award and/or post-award activities in a higher education or research environment. Demonstrated experience supporting the preparation, review, or administration of grant and contract proposals and/or awards, including familiarity with institutional and sponsor requirements. Working knowledge of governing regulations related to sponsored projects, including OMB Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200) and, as applicable, the Federal Acquisition Regulations (FAR). Experience using enterprise systems or databases related to sponsored research, finance, or administration (e.g., Workday, Cayuse, or comparable platforms), with the ability to navigate and maintain accurate records across systems. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Spring-Valley-Campus-Washington-DC/Subrecipient-and-Post-Award-Specialist_R5020": {
      "title": "Subrecipient and Post-Award Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: SO000016 Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 02 (On Campus 2 days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: This position provides specialized post-award administrative support for the institutional management of subawards and other grant-related third-party agreements associated with a designated subset of externally funded research, education, service, training, and other sponsored programs. This includes subawards, Sponsored Consulting Agreements, and grant-related Professional Services Agreements (PSAs) for which American University is the issuing party.   The Subrecipient & Post-Award Specialist serves as the institutional authority for the preparation, coordination, and ongoing administration of these agreements within American University’s systems of record, including responsibility for enforcing contractual compliance and institutional subrecipient monitoring requirements.   Operating under the functional guidance of and reporting to a designated Senior Manager of Grants & Contracts, the Subrecipient & Post-Award Specialist ensures that all grant-related third-party agreements comply with sponsor requirements, federal regulations, and AU policies and are prepared and routed for institutional review and execution by designated Authorized Organizational Representatives (AORs). The role works in close partnership with Procurement and Legal to ensure contractual integrity, regulatory flow-down, institutional alignment, and audit-ready subrecipient records across all grant-funded third-party agreements.   Essential Functions: 1.) Grant-Related Third-Party Agreement Administration Serves as the institutional authority for the creation, execution, and ongoing administration of a designated set of grant-related third-party agreements for which American University is the issuing party, including subawards, Sponsored Consulting Agreements, and grant-related Professional Services Agreements (PSAs). Creates, executes, and administers subawards and subaward amendments in accordance with sponsor requirements, federal regulations, and AU policies. Creates, executes, and administers grant-related Professional Services Agreements (PSAs) and Sponsored Consulting Agreements associated with externally funded projects. Establishes and maintains supplier contracts and related records for all grant-related third-party agreements in AU’s systems of record. Ensures appropriate flow-down of sponsor terms, regulatory requirements, and institutional policies into all grant-related third-party agreements. Processes modifications, extensions, and closeout actions for all grant-related third-party agreements. Serves as the primary ORSA point of contact for contractual and administrative matters related to grant-funded third-party agreements. Coordinates with faculty, unit administrators, Procurement, Legal, Risk, and other offices to resolve contractual or system-related issues. Maintains accurate, complete, and audit-ready institutional records for all grant-related third-party agreements. Identifies nonstandard, complex, or high-risk third-party agreement issues and escalates them to the Senior Manager for institutional review and resolution. 2.) Subrecipient Risk Management & Compliance Review Leads institutional subrecipient risk management and compliance review functions in accordance with federal regulations, sponsor requirements, and AU policies. Implements and enforces institutional subrecipient monitoring requirements under Uniform Guidance and sponsor policies. Conducts subrecipient risk assessments and supports risk-based classification and monitoring levels. Tracks required subrecipient certifications, audit documentation, and compliance attestations. Maintains institutional records related to subrecipient risk determinations and monitoring activities. Coordinates follow-up on missing, late, or noncompliant subrecipient documentation and escalates concerns as appropriate. Supports audits, sponsor reviews, and internal monitoring activities related to subrecipient compliance. Collaborates with Senior Managers, faculty, unit administrators, and central offices to resolve subrecipient compliance issues. 3.) Outreach, Training & Professional Development Participates in research administration training programs for the University community. This includes attending quarterly grant roundtable meetings and department meetings and participating in the University Research Administrator Certification Training program. Remains up-to-date with all University, federal and funder financial and budgetary guidelines related to externally funded grants and contracts. 4.) Additional Duties Additional responsibilities as assigned or requested by the Vice Provost for Research and Innovation, Director of Sponsored Awards, or Associate Director of Sponsored Awards.      Competencies: Serving Customers. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $70,000.00 - $75,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 2 - 4 years of relevant experience in contract administration, subaward administration, procurement, or a closely related field, with demonstrated responsibility for post-award or third-party agreement functions in a professional environment, including familiarity with contract terms, modifications, and closeout processes. Demonstrated ability to apply institutional policies and sponsor requirements to real-world contract and compliance scenarios with a high degree of accuracy and attention to detail. Evidence of strong organizational and time management skills, including the ability to manage multiple concurrent actions, track status, meet deadlines, and maintain audit-ready documentation. Strong technical aptitude, including the ability to work with data, contracts, and system records in a highly regulated environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with faculty, administrators, Procurement, Legal, and external partners. Demonstrated ability to work independently with minimal supervision while collaborating effectively with cross-functional teams across the institution.   Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree. Experience in higher education. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience in sponsored research administration, contract administration, subaward administration, procurement, or a closely related field, with demonstrated responsibility for post-award or third-party agreement functions in a research or higher education environment. Demonstrated experience in the preparation, administration, or coordination of subawards, third-party agreements, or grant-related contracts, including familiarity with contract terms, modifications, and closeout processes. Working knowledge of governing regulations related to sponsored projects and subawards, including OMB Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200) and, as applicable, the Federal Acquisition Regulations (FAR). Experience working with enterprise systems related to sponsored research, procurement, or finance (e.g., Workday, Cayuse, or comparable platforms) and the ability to reconcile and maintain accurate records across multiple systems.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Knowledge of academic institutions and the federal government environment. Exhibits special aptitude for critical and analytical thought, project conceptualization and examination of compliance, legal, and research issues. Demonstrated ability to maintain collegial and collaborative working relationships with faculty and staff; ability to relate to faculty member’s projects and research interests on a professional level.   Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Graduate-Dialogue--Instructional-Design-and-Leadership-Development-Assistant--Student-_R3888": {
      "title": "Graduate Dialogue, Instructional Design and Leadership Development Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Education & Support Programs Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Center for Student Belonging (CSB) is one of the key units within the Student Engagement portfolio of the Division of Student Affairs. CSB fosters an inclusive, identity-conscious, and belonging driven campus environment where students can explore their identities, build connections, and develop leadership skills. Graduate Assistants are central to the Center’s mission, helping to plan and implement programs, support students, and contribute to a campus culture that promotes belonging and community engagement. Reporting to the Associate Director, the Graduate Assistant (GA) contributes to the design, coordination, and facilitation of leadership programs, cultural engagement initiatives, and community-building experiences that strengthen students’ sense of belonging. The GA collaborates closely with CSB staff, student leaders, and campus partners to plan and execute high-impact programs. Responsibilities include supporting leadership in curriculum design, managing program logistics, mentoring student leaders, and contributing to strategic initiatives that enhance engagement and retention. Essential Functions: Design and implement leadership development programs for students. Plan and facilitate workshops, training sessions, and retreats for student leaders and student organizations. Co-create curriculum that emphasizes inclusive leadership, identity development, and fostering a sense of belonging. Support planning and execution of CSB programs and events. Assist with logistics and coordination for Center programs such as cultural heritage events, student engagement initiatives, and community-building workshops. Collaborate with student leaders and CSB staff to ensure programs are inclusive, accessible, and aligned with CSB goals. Advise and mentor student leaders. Provide guidance on program design, event management, and leadership skills. Support student leaders in reflecting their growth, navigating challenges, and achieving their programmatic goals. Supervise and coordinate student staff involvement in programs. Oversee student staff assignments for workshops and events, including scheduling, training, and on-site support. Model effective leadership and provide feedback to student staff to develop their skills. Evaluate and improve programs through data and feedback. Collect participant feedback and other assessment data to determine program effectiveness. Collaborate with staff to implement improvements to programs, workshops, and leadership initiatives. Assist with marketing and communications for programs and initiatives. Develop promotional materials, social media content, and email campaigns to increase awareness and engagement in CSB programs. Ensure communications reflect CSB’s mission and highlight student achievements. Manage resources and logistical needs for programs. Maintain inventory of materials, supplies, and equipment needed for workshops and events. Support efficient use of resources to enhance program delivery and student experience. Competencies: Advising and Supporting: Addresses the knowledge, skills, and dispositions related to providing advising and support to individuals and groups through direction, feedback, critique, referral, and guidance. Through developing advising and supporting strategies that take into account self-knowledge and the needs of others, we play critical roles in advancing the holistic wellness of ourselves, our students, and our colleagues. Social Justice and Inclusion: While there are many conceptions of social justice and inclusion in various contexts, for the purposes of this competency area, it is defined here as both a process and a goal which includes the knowledge, skills, and dispositions needed to create learning environments that foster equitable participation of all groups while seeking to address and acknowledge issues of oppression, privilege, and power. This competency involves student affairs educators who have a sense of their own agency and social responsibility that includes others, their community, and the larger global context. Student affairs educators may incorporate social justice and inclusion competencies into their practice through seeking to meet the needs of all groups, equitably distributing resources, raising social consciousness, and repairing past and current harms on campus communities. Assessment, Evaluation, and Research: The Assessment, Evaluation, and Research competency area focuses on the ability to design, conduct, critique, and use various AER methodologies and the results obtained from them, to utilize AER processes and their results to inform practice, and to shape the political and ethical climate surrounding AER processes and uses in higher education. Leadership: The Leadership competency area addresses the knowledge, skills, and dispositions required of a leader, with or without positional authority. Leadership involves both the individual role of a leader and the leadership process of individuals working together to envision, plan, and affect change in organizations and respond to broad-based constituencies and issues. This can include working with students, student affairs colleagues, faculty, and community members. Organizational and Human Resources: The Organizational and Human Resources competency area includes knowledge, skills, and aptitude used in the management of institutional human capital, financial, and physical resources. This competency area recognizes that student affairs professionals bring personal strengths and grow as managers through challenging themselves to build new skills in the selection, supervision, motivation, and formal evaluation of staff; resolution of conflict; management of the politics of organizational discourse; and the effective application of strategies and techniques associated with financial resources, facilities management, fundraising, technology, crisis management, risk management, and sustainable resources. Student Learning and Development: The Student Learning and Development competency area addresses the concepts and principles of student development and learning theory. This includes the ability to apply theory to improve and inform student affairs and teaching practice. Technology: The Technology competency area focuses on the use of digital tools, resources, and technologies for the advancement of student learning, development, and success as well as the improved performance of student affairs professionals. Included within this area are knowledge, skills, and dispositions that lead to the generation of digital literacy and digital citizenship within communities of students, student affairs professionals, faculty members, and colleges and universities. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 20 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.95 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree. Enrollment in AU’s School of Education graduate programs. Experience in student programming and/or leadership development.   Preferred Education and Experience: Experience designing or leading large-scale student programs. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to adapt to a fast-paced and changing environment. Experience working effectively with diverse constituencies. Collaborative work style and team orientation.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: This position is primarily in-person, with occasional virtual support. Flexible scheduling with an expected part-time commitment of up to 20 hours/week. Evening availability and core day availability (Monday, Wednesday, and/or Thursday). Position requires collaboration with students, staff, and campus partners. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Facilities-Operations--Student---FWS-_R5045": {
      "title": "Facilities Operations (Student) (FWS)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Athletics Operations Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Student Athletics Facilities & Operations Assistant will support the daily operations of athletic facilities, competitions, practices, meetings, and special events. This position provides hands-on experience in sports facility management and event operations while working closely with athletics staff, coaches, student-athletes, officials, vendors, and campus partners. Essential Functions: Assist with game-day and event operations, including setup, preparation, and support during competitions and special events. Set up and tear down athletic facilities for games, practices, meetings, camps, clinics, and other events. Assist with competition facility preparation, including equipment, signage, seating, tables, and other event needs. Manage and maintain inventory of athletics-owned equipment and supplies. Assist with facility checks and general upkeep to ensure athletic spaces are clean, organized, safe, and event-ready. Prepare facilities for practices, team meetings, competitions, and other scheduled activities. Support athletics staff with event logistics, including moving equipment and materials and assisting with last-minute operational needs. Provide support during home athletic competitions and other events as assigned. Perform other facility and event operations duties as assigned. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 5 - 15 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Student must have an active Federal Work Study (FWS) award. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/East-Campus-Washington-DC/Associate-or-Full-Professor-of-Interactive-Media-Film-and-Media-Arts-School-of-Communication_R5022": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Interactive Media Film and Media Arts School of Communication",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: School of Communication Time Type: Full time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: The School of Communication at American University (AU) in Washington, D.C., invites applications for a full-time, faculty position at the rank of associate or full professor, with an emphasis on interactive storytelling, worldbuilding, and/or playful design in the public interest. Depending on qualifications, the appointee may be recommended for tenure at the time of hiring. Candidates should hold a terminal degree as appropriate to their focus, such as an MFA or Ph.D. The salary range is from $105,000-$124,000.   The School of Communication (SOC) at American University engages the most pressing issues of our time through teaching, scholarship, and professional experiences in communication and media.  In the Film and Media Arts division, our faculty and students make media that matters in and beyond one of the most dynamic and influential cities in the world.   The candidate is expected to work closely with or lead American University’s Game & Interactive Media Center, which is a world-class hub for experiential education, interactive storytelling, and computational media with a public purpose. While the Center is increasingly focused on undergraduate learning, its graduate programs were ranked #24 in the world by the Princeton Review in 2026, including the MA in Game Design and the MFA in Games and Interactive Media. The Center recently launched a new minor in Game Design, with one track for media studies students and another for those with an interest in computer science and game development.   The ideal candidate will be an outstanding media maker, educator, and curricular strategist. They should ideally be comfortable with many principles of good game design. The candidate’s practice in interactive media should connect to one or more domains in the public interest: the environment, health, urban and spatial planning, education, or social issue storytelling, and should interface with science communication and journalism, museums, nonprofits, etc. As more AI tools are integrated into production pipelines, the candidate should have some knowledge of where such tools are useful and how they can be guided, and be able to engage thoughtfully with students who are both AI resistant and AI enthusiasts. Job responsibilities may include teaching courses; directing undergraduate and graduate game programs; mentoring students; securing new partnerships and funding for original media projects and high-impact collaborations; and participating in School of Communication and University governance.   The position, which will begin in August of 2027, comes with powerful opportunities for collaboration with a tremendous mix of organizations in Washington, DC, from arts funders to policy think tanks to global nonprofits. Collaborating across the university with our world-class faculty is particularly well-suited to the Game & Interactive Media Center and is encouraged.   Consideration of applications will commence on November 1, 2026. Candidate interviews will begin in late 2026/early 2027 and continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae, one letter of recommendation (two more letters will be requested for those who reach the interview stage), a teaching portfolio, optional creative portfolio, and optional excerpt of exemplary publications; finalist candidates will be asked for two letters of recommendation (likely to coincide with interviews).  Please submit applications via Workday. Please contact the SOC Faculty Affairs Coordinator at 202-885-2133 or email facultyaffairs_SOC@american.edu if you have any questions.      For more about the School of Communication, please visit www.american.edu/soc . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively “Protected Bases”) in its programs and activities.   American University is a tobacco- and smoke-free campus. Hiring offers for this position are contingent on the successful completion of a background check.   American University is required to comply with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). In accordance with the Clery Act, each year American University prepares an Annual Security Report. This report contains campus crime statistics as well as descriptions of Public Safety's services. It is available to all applicants upon request by calling the Department of Public Safety at 202-885-2537 or the Annual Security Report is also available online. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Adjunct-Faculty---CAS-Department-of-Physics_R958": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - CAS Department of Physics (Open Continuous)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nPart time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: College of Arts & Sciences Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: This is an open continuous vacancy announcement that will be used to fill adjunct vacancies as they occur. Until you hear from a representative of the Department of Physics or the Office of HR Faculty, please continue to also apply directly to other positions posted on AU's website. The Department of Physics in the College of Arts and Sciences at American University invites applications for a number of adjunct faculty positions for upcoming Academic Years, specifically the Spring 2027 semester. Rank will depend on an applicant’s highest degree earned in Physics or a related field. The applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The Department of Physics offers undergraduate degree programs. The Department is organized under the College of Arts and Sciences, which is where scholarship in the Arts, Humanities, and Sciences is pursued. Learn more about the College of Arts and Sciences at https://www.american.edu/cas/ and the Department of Physics at https://www.american.edu/cas/physics/ . The Department of Physics in the College of Arts and Sciences has the following adjunct salary ranges: Candidates Without a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $3,130 3-4 Credit Course: $5,163 5-6 Credit Course: $5,395 Candidates With a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $3,730 3-4 Credit Course: $5,900 5-6 Credit Course: $6,062 Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/4401-Connecticut-Campus-Washington-DC/Assistant-Dean-of-Development--SPA_R2730": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean of Development, SPA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Development - Schools & Colleges Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: Job Description: Summary: The Assistant Dean of Development, School of Public Affairs (“SPA”) is the principal fundraiser for SPA at American University.  The Assistant Dean of Development works on behalf of the Dean of SPA, shaping and executing plans for fundraising to meet the financial needs of the Dean’s academic priorities for the School.  The Assistant Dean of Development works primarily in the area of major gift fundraising for SPA and participates in the Dean’s senior management team in order to understand fully and discuss the implications of academic programs for development.  The Assistant Dean of Development plans, implements, and oversees the major gift fundraising program for SPA with emphasis on gifts of $100,000 to $1,000,000+, as well as collaborates with the principal gifts program, parent philanthropy, annual giving, as well as foundation/corporate giving.  The Assistant Dean of Development coordinates closely with the Senior Director of Development, Annual Giving to ensure messages and priorities are consistent for the mail and phone programs; as well as with directors in other areas (Planned Giving, Leadership Annual Giving, Strategic Partnerships, other units) on mutual interest prospects and programs. Essential Functions: 1.) Major Gift Strategy With the Dean and independently, aggressively work major gifts portfolio under the prospect development guidelines, actively setting and executing strategies on assigned major gift prospects, meeting or exceeding established benchmarks for visits, contacts, and solicitations, and securing gifts that support the academic priorities of the Dean, other units, or the University as a whole.             2.) Lead Fundraising Strategy  Develop, execute, manage, fundraising strategies, priorities, schedules and tactics for the Dean of the School of Public Affairs, including but not limited to: Identifying and engaging prospects around the Dean’s academic priorities for the School. Planning travel and individual meetings. Managing regular communication with key prospects. Adding a development perspective to relevant matters that emerge in the School or on the Dean’s leadership team. Managing all aspects of the Dean’s Advocacy Council, including developing strategy to cultivate and solicit council members for leadership giving at SPA. Management and strategy development of a pool of 150 assigned, rated, and staged prospects generally rated $100,000-$1,000,000+. Meaningful contact or consistently demonstrated attempted contact of 100% of the assigned portfolio during any six month period. Regular maintenance of prospect pipeline (i.e., planned and executed solicitations) to reflect a minimum of 15 major gift solicitations per year. Varying by stage of cultivation, consistent movement of prospects in assigned portfolio in prescribed time frame. (As an example, it is expected that prospects will only be in qualification and early cultivation mode for 6 months, regular cultivation for 12 months, and pre-solicitation cultivation and consideration mode for 6 months). A minimum of 10 planned, strategic visits per month with many other interactions, meetings, events, and unplanned visits. Regular updates to contacts, staging, ratings so that all reports are current for supervision meetings and monthly progress reports. Work in concert with University Advancement colleagues, including particularly close collaboration with the Planned Giving Team, and Office of Prospect Development to conduct entrepreneurial prospect identification and qualification work equal to approximately 10-20% of all activity.  (More if Assistant Dean of Development meets expectations of progress with assigned portfolio). The Assistant Dean of Development’s financial objectives will be established collaboratively and with close review of the capacity and readiness indicators of the Assistant Dean of Development’s prospect portfolio.             3.) Lead and Manage Team Members Manage, motivate, and evaluate the School of Public Affairs Development team:  Director of Development, SPA and Development Coordinator, SPA. Develop major gift strategies and revenue expectations, donor stewardship initiatives, and special event plans. Delegate and assign tasks, including creation and design of related publications.              4.) Coordinate Annual Giving Work with AU’s Annual Giving team to effectively manage annual giving techniques and messages utilized in AU’s direct marketing and phone programs, assuming responsibility for making sure the information about the School of Public Affairs used in these programs is accurate, up to date, and in line with the School’s current fundraising objectives. Work closely with the Director of Development, Annual Giving, on annual Dean’s letter or other equivalent solicitation pieces, insuring that they occupy a reasonable and productive spot in AU’s direct marketing calendar.             5.) Other Duties As Assigned    Supervisory Responsibility: This position supervises FT staff members.             Competencies: Building and Supporting Teams. Acting Strategically. Developing Plans. Managing Performance. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-Time. 35 hr/week. Position also requires occasional evening and weekend work. Salary Range: $150,000 - $170,000 annually. Required Education and Experience Bachelor's degree or equivalent from an accredited educational institution. 8-10 years of relevant experience. Eight or more years progressively responsible development experience required, with at least four years in demonstrated success in securing major gifts of $100,000+. Familiarity with and experience following guidelines of an established Prospect Development system; computer literacy with MS Office suite or equivalent and familiarity with fundraising systems; superior written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Education and Experience Master's degree or equivalent. 8-10 years of relevant experience. Experience in higher education, working with high-level executives/academics/leaders, managing volunteers, major gift solicitation and closure at the $500,000+ level.  Attention to detail, ability to analyze and articulate reasons for giving, strong priority setting, ability to manage multiple tasks simultaneously in a deadline-oriented environment. Maturity and diplomacy. Analytical, research, and project management skills.  Travel Required: This position requires regular local travel, and out of area travel approximately 25-40% of time.  Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Assistant-Director--Student-Affairs-Assessment-and-Planning_R5014": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Student Affairs Assessment and Planning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Student Affairs Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Full On Campus Presence Union: Excluded Job Description: Summary: The Assistant Director, Student Affairs Assessment and Planning serves as a strategic leader in advancing a culture of assessment, evidence-based decision-making, and continuous improvement across the Division of Student Affairs. The Assistant Director, Student Affairs Assessment and Planning partners with all Student Affairs departments to enhance programmatic and operational outcomes, strengthen student learning and engagement, and ensure alignment with institutional and divisional strategic priorities. This role provides leadership in the coordination, development, and implementation of comprehensive assessment, evaluation, and planning processes that drive informed decision-making and innovation. The Assistant Director, Student Affairs Assessment and Planning leads data collection, analysis, and reporting for divisional initiatives. As a key advisor to Vice Provost for Student Affairs, this role provides insight on trends and outcomes related to student belonging, engagement, and success, informing strategies that promote meaningful student experiences and equitable outcomes. The position requires strong collaboration with the office of Institutional Research, as well as with campus partners and active engagement with students, families, and external stakeholders to advance Student Affairs priorities.   Essential Functions:   1.) Assessment Planning and Coordination Provides strategic leadership in the design, implementation, and coordination of comprehensive assessment plans across all Student Affairs departments. Ensures assessment initiatives are aligned with divisional priorities, institutional goals, and accreditation standards. Partners with divisional leaders and departments to develop and refine learning outcomes, performance indicators, and measurable goals that strengthen program quality, operational effectiveness, and the student experience. Analyzes assessment results to identify trends, highlight opportunities for continuous improvement, and recommend evidence-based enhancements to divisional practices, policies, and procedures. Develop and manage systems for tracking and evaluating divisional initiatives, strategic priorities, and deliverables to promote accountability and progress toward measurable student success outcomes. Leads the creation and maintenance of an annual Student Affairs assessment and planning calendar, ensuring timely reporting and integration with university-wide assessment cycles. Leads the Division’s assessment committee. Leads the production of the annual Division of Student Affairs Annual Impact Reports, department annual reports, and semester reports. Provide leadership, coordination and project management with the division’s strategic action plan. Collaborate with key campus partners including the Office of Information Technology, Institutional Research, Student Retention and Success, Undergraduate Education and Academic Services, and Enrollment Management to support shared projects, data integration efforts, and other-related initiatives. 2.) Data Collection, Analysis, and Reporting Collects, analyzes, and interprets strategic data to inform divisional decision-making, resource allocation, and long-term planning. Provides actionable insights that shape programmatic assessment, strategic initiatives, and emerging Student Affairs priorities. Develops and implements strategies to identify and engage students not currently involved in co-curricular activities. Provide assessment support for Housing & Residence Life, including the Residential Experience and the Residential Curriculum. Support assessment planning related to accreditation and continuous quality improvement for both the Student Health Center and the Center for Well-Being. Support division-wide assessment, data analytics, dashboards, program evaluation, survey administration, and institutional reporting to support strategic planning and executive decision-making. Serve as the point of contact for Student Affairs student success outcomes, and supports all major cohorts, programs and initiatives that tell the division’s student success stories. Collaborates with key partners across campus including Institutional Research, Undergraduate Education, and Student Retention and Success to design data-informed interventions that increase engagement, belonging, and retention. In conjunction with the Student Affairs Data Analytics Team, oversees the data analysis and presentation of assessment projects conducted across Student Affairs departments, ensuring findings are clearly communicated, actionable, and aligned with institutional priorities. Prepares and delivers high-quality reports and presentations for Board of Trustees, senior leadership, university committees, and external audiences, articulating progress toward divisional goals and institutional impact.   3.) Consultation, Training and Capacity Building Serves as the division’s subject matter expert on Student Affairs assessment, benchmarking, and evaluation best practices, ensuring divisional strategies align with national standards and institutional goals. Creates and standardizes templates, policies, and procedures for internal and external reporting to enhance consistency, transparency, and efficiency in data management and communication across the division. Provides expert training and consultation to divisional staff on assessment design, data collection, analysis, interpretation, and reporting. Supports departments in building the capacity to use evidence-based decision-making to enhance programs and student outcomes. Develops and facilitates workshops, guides, and digital resources to strengthen staff assessment literacy and promote a culture of continuous improvement across the Division of Student Affairs. Oversee the development and maintenance of the Student Affairs Assessment website, ensuring it serves as a dynamic resource for data sharing, transparency, and communication of divisional impact.    4.) Collaboration, Partnerships and Student Activism Support Team Serves as a designated member of the Student Activism Support Lead Team (SAST) and participates in an on-call rotation to support student activism, expressive activity, demonstrations, advocacy efforts, and other student-led engagement activities. Serves as a visible university representative and resource during planned and emergent student activities, promoting constructive engagement, effective communication, conflict de-escalation, and adherence to university policies and community standards. Provides after-hours and weekend support for student programs, campus events, demonstrations, and other activities that may require university response, coordination, or intervention on alternating basis. Collaborates with campus partners including the AU Police Department, Office of Student Accountability and Restorative Practices, the Office of Inclusive Excellence, and other university stakeholders to support student well-being, campus operations, and institutional response efforts. Maintains availability during assigned on-call periods to respond to emerging recognized student organization events and/or activism concerns, assess situations, provide appropriate support and referrals, and ensure continuity of university services during evenings, weekends, holidays, and other non-business hours. Coordinate with other Student Affairs and University departments, and external organizations to conceive, develop, and execute initiatives and programs. Establish and maintain collaborative working relationships with offices on campus to promote student engagement, success, and retention. Support major division and university-wide programs, including but not limited to, Commencement, Family Weekend, Move-In, New Student Orientation, Family and Supporters Orientation, Student Achievement Awards, and Eagles at Night. As a member of the Division of Student Affairs, this position may advise recognized student organizations as needed.   5.) Projects and Support Leads the Student Affairs Student Worker Learning Outcomes Project, overseeing the design, implementation, and evaluation of outcomes-based learning experiences for student employees across the division. Serves as the Division’s project manager for projects as assigned the Vice Provost for Student Affairs. Collaborate and lead the design and implementation VPSA communications, including but not limited to assessment updates, progress reports, and data storytelling initiatives that elevate the visibility and impact of Student Affairs work. Coordinate divisional program reviews with individual units based upon the respective standards of the functional area being reviewed. 6.) Supervision Hires, trains, and supervises 1 – 2 Graduate Assistants. Provides an environment conducive to intellectual curiosity, effective challenge and support, and teamwork. Meets with staff member(s) individually to discuss their performance and provide professional development opportunities.   Supervisory Responsibility: Supervises 1 - 2 Graduate Assistants.   Competencies: Serving Customers. Displaying Creativity. Prioritizing and Organizing. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Full on campus presence. This position has non-traditional hours, 11am - 7pm, Monday - Friday. Staff will work on an adjusted work schedule as needed, including late nights and weekends to support divisional programs, Student Activism Support Team, and other activities.  Salary Range: $63,160.00 - $66,900.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 2 - 4 years of relevant experience.   Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree or equivalent. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience. Preferred experience in the field of higher education institutional research or in higher education data analysis.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Inclusive Excellence: An ability to welcome, value, and affirm individuals of all identities and experiences, and a commitment to ensure they are understood, appreciated, and fully included in the university community. Discretion, Tact and Diplomacy: Ability to exercise discretion, tact and diplomacy while interacting with students, faculty, university staff, management, internship employers and other stakeholders on confidential and/or sensitive matters. Advanced Communication: Ability to express oneself clearly and concisely, using language with precision; constructing logical arguments; accurate note taking, editing and summarizing; and writing reports or correspondence free of spelling and grammatical errors. Ability to engage in active and empathetic listening and communicate with a service minded orientation.    Thoroughness in Reviewing Work: Ability to review one’s own and others work and information to ensure completeness and accuracy; carefully prepare for meetings and presentations; organize information or materials for others; follow up with others to ensure that agreements and commitments have been fulfilled; and set up procedures to ensure high quality of work. Student Services Skills: Ability to serve as bridge to services, intervene and/or advocate on students’ behalf with offices across campus to support needs, drive engagement and empower students to achieve their goals. Diagnostic Information Gathering: Ability to seek out relevant people to obtain information; probe skillfully to get at the facts when others are reluctant to disclose the full details; ask clarifying questions; and gain a complete and accurate picture by checking multiple sources. Standard Office Software: Skill using email, word processing, spreadsheet/ data analysis and presentation development software applications such as Microsoft Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint while preparing briefings, reports, and presentations.   Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Online-Graduate-Enrollment-Lead_R5015": {
      "title": "Online Graduate Enrollment Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: School of Public Affairs Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead is a new and integral, position in the Office of Graduate Admissions (OGA) at the School of Public Affairs (SPA). The primary purview of the position is to support the development and execution of a comprehensive, data-driven, online recruitment strategy, manage the application, admissions, and merit aid allocation, conduct timely yield events and initiatives and lead as well as contribute to reporting for SPA’s Master of Public Administration and Policy (MPAP) and MS in Counter-Terrorism and Homeland Security (CTHS) degrees. The incumbent will be required to establish and prioritize a high-touch, personalized, and mission-aligned enrollment lifecycle experience for all interested students (prospect to enrollee).   The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead will serve as the CRM lead. They will be responsible for making full use of the campus’ Customer Relationship Management (CRM) platform, Salesforce (SF), as well as the Salesforce Marketing Cloud (SFMC) to support the online enrollment cycle. In addition to the CRM’s management of the application, admissions, and merit aid processes, the incumbent is charged with the implementation and use of a Virtual Agent, a technology capability in SF that will function as a \"digital team member\" enhancing response time and driving operational efficacy. The SFMC will be leveraged for the release of communications, establishing communication workflows, as well as creating dedicated degree journeys for SPA’s online programs.   With a supervisory line to the School’s Assistant Dean of Graduate Enrollment, the Online Graduate Enrollment Lead will leverage established strategy, processes, and reporting tools, when applicable, in the OGA and will work closely with the associated staff in aligning online operations as well as administration. The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead’s purview also requires close and detailed work with SPA’s online Faculty Program Directors, OGA team members, online advisor(s), SPA Communications & Marketing, Office of Graduate and Professional Studies (OGPS), Office of Information Technology (OIT), and, most importantly, valued online prospective students.   As an important part of the OGA, the Online Graduate Enrollment Lead will also, on occasion, be requested to contribute to operational projects and/or reporting as well as participate in (and, as pertinent, lead) major recruitment events (online and on campus), virtual sessions, relevant School, campus, and team meetings, as well as required and desired professional development initiatives. Essential Functions: 1.) Online Graduate Recruitment, Communication, & Events The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead is responsible for leveraging expertise in online student recruitment to create and execute a data-driven strategic recruitment plan, developing targeted initiatives that drive high-quality inquiries and applications. The recruitment plan should be grounded by landscape research and market analysis to identify relevant trends to ensure the programs’ competitive and unique positioning in the market at large as well as around SPA’s top-ranked, comparable on-campus degree offerings.  And, in collaboration with SPA Communications & Marketing, develop a complementary, sophisticated multi-channel communication plan designed to nurture a diverse, high-quality cohort of online students for the MPAP and CTHSP degrees. The internal communication plan should leverage the SFMC and, as much as viable for the online market segment, align with SPA’s communication strategy for the School’s on-campus programs. The incumbent is also responsible for designing, implementing, and leading, as relevant, virtual information sessions (i.e., recruitment, application coaching, Post-Admission Workshops), establishing regular \"Live Chat\" opportunities that allow prospective students to interact with faculty and current students, and instituting relevant yield events (i.e., virtual topical sessions, Admit Day and/or Open Houses).   2.) Admissions & Graduate Merit Aid In consultation and collaboration with the Assistant Dean and Online Program Directors, administers the faculty-driven admissions and merit award processes for SPA’s online graduate programs. Leads the cultivation and timely processing of graduate applications and rendering of decisions for SPA’s online programs. This includes establishing recurring initiatives to support applicants in completing applications and prioritizing responsiveness to address questions and concerns from online applicants, as well as faculty directors and related staff regarding the SPA online program application/admissions processes. Updates and maintains the School’s online admission committee lists and relevant, degree-specific admission criteria. In collaboration with the assistant dean, serves as a primary point of contact for the online faculty program directors in matters related to admissions processes and policies. Conducts admission interviews for online applicants as requested. Supports the online graduate program merit aid process by releasing award-specific communications as well as tracking, reporting,  auditing, and loading awards for all new/incoming online graduate students for each entry term.   3.) Graduate Enrollment Reporting & Analytics Leads, contributes, and provides analysis of online graduate enrollment data. This requires leveraging the university enterprise system (Microstrategy Business Intelligence (BI)) and the campus’ CRM, Salesforce. The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead will support the current, weekly BI reporting in the OGA, lead responses to ad hoc online data requests, and contribute to OGA’s monthly Graduate Enrollment Report as well as the annual, comprehensive analytics report(s) to/for School- and University-based stakeholders.   4.) CRM Lead Serves as the CRM Lead for SPA’s online graduate programs, working closely with the OGA’s Associate Director of Graduate Admissions & Enrollment Analytics, Office of Information Technology (OIT), Office of Graduate and Professional Studies (OGPS), and other CRM Leads, as needed, across the AU campus. The position is responsible for troubleshooting system issues, testing post-production upgrades and/or updates, training, as needed, of SPA faculty, staff, and student workers associated with SPA’s online programs, and serving as the primary point of contact for users (including online prospective students as they interface with the application portal). Serves as the CRM expert as it relates to online graduate program enrollment processes including application and admission management, view and dashboard development, advanced queries, workflow implementation and modification, and reporting. Coordinates with OGA’s Associate Director of Graduate Admissions and Enrollment Analytics in adhering to University/School data security and confidentiality policies and in maintaining the CRM access and users list.   5.) Supervision & Operational Support The Online Graduate Enrollment Lead will be expected to manage the designated Graduate Student Ambassador(s) and Virtual Agent prioritizing work that supports online recruitment, admissions, and yield activities. The work will include forward-facing communications, telephone and email campaigns, and “Chats” (email, telephone, and virtual), as well as various research projects, and other pertinent initiatives. It is anticipated that the deployment of the Virtual Agent technology in support of the online graduate enrollment cycle will require enhanced start-up measures. (Additional support for the Online Graduate Enrollment Lead with this technology will be deployed within the OGA and, ideally, the campus’ OIT.) The incumbent will also be expected to support OGA operational initiatives, events, and projects as relevant and as capacity allows.   6.) Other Duties as Assigned Supervisory Responsibility: Will supervise a graduate student(s) and virtual agent to support online enrollment operation. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $70,000.00 - $75,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent in public affairs/policy, education, mathematics, statistics, computer science, economics, science, social science, business, or a related field required. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience providing analytical support or working in a higher education/admission setting. Technical and strong Excel proficiency required. Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree in education, mathematics, statistics, computer science, economics, science, social science, business, or a related field preferred. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Experience with Customer Relationship Management platforms and, specifically, dashboard and reporting capabilities. Ability to formulate inquiries and analysis to inform and advance an enrollment operation. Ability to work collaboratively with campus-level graduate enrollment professionals and technical support. Ability to prioritize tasks and manage competing priorities on deadline. Ability to learn and successfully use new software programs as needed. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision. Attention to detail and to manage confidential student information/data. Excellent customer service and critical thinking skills. Excellent communication skills (written and oral). Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Spring-Valley-Campus-Washington-DC/Principal-Outreach-Partner_R5018": {
      "title": "Principal Outreach Partner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Research Services & Communications Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 02 (On Campus 2 days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Principal Outreach Partner serves as ORSA’s dedicated internal communicator, service partner, and campus connector for the AU research community. Reporting to the Director of Strategy and Communications, this position ensures that faculty, staff, and unit-based research teams can effectively access, navigate, and utilize the university’s research support services. The role is centered on internal communications, service delivery, and practical application—helping the research community understand how to apply institutional policies, procedures, and user guidance in real-world contexts, including those related to Institutional Review Board (IRB) protocols, animal welfare compliance, human subjects research protections, and data security requirements. A core function of this position is the development and production of internal research communications, including a monthly funding opportunities and research development newsletter, and maintaining the research administration website. The Principal Outreach Partner Research writes, formats, and packages content—along with research development resources and institutional updates—into clear, accessible communications for the campus research community. The incumbent also serves as a hands-on resource for faculty and staff who need additional guidance beyond formal training and documentation. Working in a consultative, tutorial capacity, the Principal Outreach Partner helps users interpret and apply the tools, systems, and resources produced by the ORSA team—providing one-on-one and small-group support through walkthroughs, Q&A sessions, and practical demonstrations. This role reinforces adoption by meeting users where they are and translating institutional requirements into clear, contextual guidance. The incumbent develops the canonical documentation, SOPs, and training materials, this position focuses on user-facing communications and direct engagement that help the research community put those resources into practice. Acting as a bridge between ORSA the Office of Research and schools, departments, centers, and institutes, the incumbent provides responsive support tailored to the needs of different disciplines and units. Through ongoing engagement across campus, the Principal Outreach Partner identifies service gaps, recurring challenges, and opportunities to improve how research support is delivered and communicated. Essential Functions: 1.) Internal Communications for Research Community Develop, write, and produce ORSA’s monthly funding opportunities and research development newsletter. Write and format newsletter content—drawing on funding opportunities identified and curated by graduate student assistant—to communicate upcoming deadlines, research development resources, institutional updates, and highlights of available support services in an accessible, audience-appropriate format. Draft and distribute clear, audience-appropriate internal communications that help faculty and staff understand and act on policies, procedures, and system changes—including those related to IRB protocols, human subjects research protections, animal welfare compliance, and data security requirements. Develop service-oriented communications—including “what you need to know” briefs, service announcements, policy update summaries, and plain-language explainers—tailored to the needs of different campus units. Proactively communicate updates related to research services, compliance requirements, institutional processes, and available resources through appropriate internal channels. Responsible for actively updating webpages to ensure content remains accurate, current, and aligned with user needs and organizational priorities. 2.) Unit Engagement & Relationship Management Serve as the primary ORSA liaison to school- and unit-based research administration teams, building strong and collaborative relationships with faculty, Associate Deans for Research (ADRs), and research administrative staff across the university. Build and maintain strong, collaborative relationships across schools, departments, centers, and institutes to understand unit-specific service needs, communication preferences, and operational challenges. Maintain a consistent and visible presence through unit meetings, office hours, workshops, and research forums. Act as a trusted, responsive point of contact for faculty and staff seeking guidance on research-related services, processes, and institutional partners. Connect faculty and staff to the appropriate offices, services, and tools, ensuring efficient coordination across the research support ecosystem. Foster cross-unit collaboration and knowledge-sharing to strengthen the overall research support community.-Establish regular points of contact with ADRs, unit research administrators, and other key stakeholders to understand school-based needs, challenges, and priorities. Maintain a visible presence through unit meetings, school-level workshops, office hours, and research forums to ensure ongoing engagement. Act as a trusted advisor and responsive point of contact for faculty and staff seeking guidance on research administration processes, available resources, and appropriate institutional partners. Foster cross-unit knowledge-sharing and strengthen collaboration among research administrators across schools and central offices. 3.) Communication & Change Management Communicate upcoming changes to policies, systems, and processes—including updates to IRB procedures, human subjects protections, animal welfare protocols, and data security standards—through internal channels, helping faculty and staff understand what is changing, why, and how it affects their work. (Note: Updated training materials, system guides, and formal documentation related to changes are produced by the ORSA Team.) Coordinate with ORSA and other subject matter experts to ensure change communications accurately reflect updated guidance, timelines, and resources. Contribute to fostering a shared understanding of research administration practices aligned with institutional priorities. Translate research policies, procedures, and system updates into clear, accessible guidance for faculty and staff. Support the culture shift around research and research administration in terms of R1 status and necessity for research administration support Proactively communicate updates related to: funding opportunities and tools, policy or regulatory changes, process improvements within the Office of Research and Sponsored Awards, and contribute to outreach activities that increase awareness of ORSA services and strengthen connections among faculty, unit staff, and external stakeholders. 4.) Service Feedback & Continuous Improvement Gather feedback from faculty, staff, and unit-based research personnel to identify service gaps, recurring challenges, and opportunities for improvement. Provide structured insights and recommendations to ORSA leadership to inform process improvements, training priorities, and resource development. Surface recurring questions and pain points that indicate where internal communications, user resources, or documentation should be created, revised, or redistributed. Identify patterns in and challenges to help improve service delivery, communications clarity, and accessibility. 5.) Onboarding & Community Support Support onboarding of new faculty and research support staff by delivering welcome communications, guided introductions, and personalized orientations to available services, processes, and institutional resources—including an overview of IRB, human subjects, animal welfare, and data security requirements and where to find related guidance. Reinforce existing training and guidance by providing follow-up support, clarification, and real-time assistance as users begin applying processes. Promote awareness and effective use of research tools and services (e.g., compliance systems, funding databases, library resources) through direct engagement. Contribute to building a more connected, informed, and service-oriented research support community across campus. 6.) User Support & Applied Guidance Provide hands-on, consultative support to faculty and staff who need additional help applying institutional policies, procedures, systems, and tools in their day-to-day research work, including IRB submission processes, human subjects protocols, animal welfare procedures, and data security practices. Serve as a tutorial resource for users by conducting one-on-one and small-group walkthroughs, Q&A sessions, and practical demonstrations of research compliance and administrative processes and systems. Reinforce and support the adoption of training, tools, and resources developed by ORSA by helping users interpret and apply them in practice. Compile and share Q&A summaries, common-issue roundups, and tip sheets drawn from recurring user questions and support interactions to help the broader research community benefit from individual consultations. (Note: Formal reference materials, job aids, and system documentation are developed and maintained by ORSA.) Connect faculty and staff to the appropriate offices, services, and tools, ensuring efficient coordination across the research support ecosystem. Troubleshoot barriers affecting research activities and workflows, and facilitate resolution in collaboration with relevant offices. Competencies: Serving Customers. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Developing Plans. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Championing Customer Needs. Displaying Creativity. Building and Supporting Teams. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $75,000.00 - $80,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 3 - 5 years of professional experience in internal communications, research administration, higher education support services, or a related area. Experience working in a university research office or with faculty-facing services would be a strong differentiator. Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. Familiarity with research compliance concepts (IRB, human subjects, animal welfare, data security) is preferred. Experience with stakeholder engagement, change management, communications, or cross-functional coordination. Experience using data or service metrics to assess needs, identify gaps, and support operational improvement. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Strong written communication skills with the ability to translate complex policies and procedures into clear, accessible language. Experience producing newsletters or internal communications. Comfort working in a consultative, one-on-one support capacity. Ability to build relationships across organizational units with diverse stakeholders. Proficiency with email marketing/newsletter platforms, Microsoft Office, and content management tools. Experience with research administration systems is a plus but not essential. Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to tailor support to varied disciplines, unit structures, and levels of research administration experience. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects and respond effectively in a service-oriented environment. Strong judgment, discretion, and commitment to confidentiality. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Adjunct-Faculty---CAS-Department-of-Philosophy-and-Religion_R957": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - CAS Department of Philosophy and Religion (Open Continuous)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: College of Arts & Sciences Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: This is an open continuous vacancy announcement that will be used to fill adjunct vacancies as they occur. Until you hear from a representative of the Department of Philosophy and Religion or the Office of HR Faculty, please continue to also apply directly to other positions posted on AU's website. The Department of Philosophy and Religion in the College of Arts and Sciences at American University invites applications for a number of adjunct faculty positions for upcoming Academic Years. Rank will depend on an applicant’s highest degree earned in Philosophy and Religion or a related field. The applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The Department of Philosophy and Religion offers both undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Department is organized under the College of Arts and Sciences, which is where scholarship in the Arts, Humanities, and Sciences is pursued. Learn more about the College of Arts and Sciences at https://www.american.edu/cas/ and the Department of Philosophy and Religion at https://www.american.edu/cas/philrel/ . The Department of Philosophy and Religion in the College of Arts and Sciences has the following adjunct salary ranges: Candidates Without a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $2,865 3-4 Credit Course: $4,725 5-6 Credit Course: $4,938 Candidates With a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $3,414 3-4 Credit Course: $5,400 5-6 Credit Course: $5,548 Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
