    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/ENVS---Social-Media-Support--Student-_R5105": {
      "title": "ENVS ​​Social Media Support (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: College of Arts & Sciences Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Social Media Coordinator will support the Department’s communications by managing our Instagram presence and producing a monthly e-newsletter. The ideal candidate is a sharp writer with an eye for visuals, organized, proactive, and comfortable collaborating with faculty and students to source content that reflects our department’s teaching, research, and community. Essential Functions: 1.) Instagram Management Maintain a consistent posting cadence (feed, Stories, Reels as appropriate) aligned with departmental priorities and the university brand. Plan a monthly content calendar; draft captions; select/edit photos or short video; schedule posts; monitor comments and DMs. Track basic analytics (reach, engagement, follower growth) and provide brief monthly summaries with recommendations. 2.) Content Sourcing & Coordination Work with the faculty supervisor to collect photos, updates, event announcements, student/faculty spotlights, and research highlights. Obtain permissions/credits for images and ensure accessibility (alt text, clear typography). 3.) Monthly Newsletter Compile and publish a department newsletter (template provided) featuring student research, faculty news, events, job/internship opportunities, and alumni highlights. Maintain a simple content tracker (submissions, deadlines, approvals); proofread for accuracy and tone; distribute via the provided platform. 4.) Event & Brand Support As needed, provide light coverage of on-campus events (e.g., one or two photos, short recap) and ensure all outputs follow AU communications and accessibility guidelines. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 10 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Current American University undergraduate in good academic standing. Strong written and visual communication skills; confident, concise caption writing. Demonstrated experience with Instagram (posting, Stories/Reels, tagging, alt text) and basic analytics. Proficiency with common design and scheduling tools (e.g., Canva/Adobe). Excellent organization and time management; able to hit recurring monthly and weekly deadlines. Professionalism, attention to detail, and comfort coordinating with faculty, staff, and students. Additional Eligibility Qualifications:  To apply, please submit: Resume, 2–3 sample posts or short-form pieces you’ve created or links to relevant work (can be coursework), a brief statement (≤200 words) describing your vision for our department’s social media presence and 2–3 accounts you find inspiring (tell us why). Please also state if you are Federal Work Study or not. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Housing---Residence-Life-Social-Media-Assistant--Student-_R5112": {
      "title": "Housing & Residence Life Social Media Assistant (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: HRL Communications Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Housing & Residence Life Marketing & Communications Assistant will support communications efforts with a focus on social media, storytelling, and event promotion. Under the supervision of the Marketing & Communications Coordinator, the assistant will gain hands-on experience in content creation, campaign management, and digital engagement strategies while contributing to initiatives that directly impact the student residential experience. Essential Functions: 1.) Social Media Management Assist in managing Housing and Residence Life’s official social media accounts (primarily Instagram), including content creation, caption writing, and scheduling posts. Capture and curate high-quality photos and videos at department events (e.g., Signature Programs, community events, and tabling). Draft and schedule weekly posts highlighting upcoming housing deadlines, student engagement opportunities, and residential life updates. Engage with followers by monitoring comments, mentions, and tags to maintain an approachable and informative online presence. 2.) Content Creation & Campaign Development Collaborate on creative campaigns promoting Housing processes such as Returning Student Room Selection and community engagement initiatives. Brainstorm and develop new content ideas that align with departmental goals and seasonal themes (e.g., room selection tips, student spotlights, or program recaps). Design digital materials such as social media graphics, story templates, and short-form videos using Canva or similar tools. 3.) Analytics & Reporting Track social media engagement metrics (views, likes, saves, followers, etc.) to evaluate performance and identify trends. Assist in preparing monthly social media performance summaries for internal review. Provide recommendations for improving engagement and reach based on data insights. 4.) Departmental Engagement Support on-site coverage of Signature Programs and major departmental events. Ensure content accurately reflects the department’s mission, values, and inclusive approach to community building. Participate in brainstorming sessions with the Marketing & Communications Coordinator to align messaging with department priorities. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 7 - 10 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Full-time enrollment as an undergraduate student at American University (minimum 12 credits and a maximum of 17 per semester). Cumulative GPA at, or above, 2.50 and term GPA at, or above, 2.50 (GPAs must be maintained from the date of application until the date of separation). Must be in good conduct standing with the university. Prior social media management experience or equivalent coursework education is required. Preferred Education and Experience: Familiarity with American University's residential communities. Knowledge of Canva. Knowledge of video editing software i.e iMovie, CapCut, etc. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Shifts are scheduled during office hours: 9AM - 5PM, Monday - Friday with some non-traditional hours for after-hour events. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Tenley-Campus-Washington-DC/Program-Assistant--Hubert-H-Humphrey-Fellowship-Program-WCL--Student-_R5109-1": {
      "title": "Program Assistant, Hubert H. Humphrey Fellowship Program WCL (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Washington College of Law Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: The Hubert H. Humphrey Fellowship Program at the Washington College of Law (WCL) is seeking a highly organized and self-motivated Program Assistant (PA/Graduate Assistant/Student Employee) with strong interpersonal and cross-cultural communication skills. The ideal candidate will have a genuine interest in global leadership development and learning about diverse cultures, as well as the ability to manage multiple priorities in a dynamic work environment. The role requires initiative, attention to detail, and professionalism in working both independently and collaboratively as part of a small team. The Hubert H. Humphrey Fellowship Program is a Fulbright Exchange activity funded by the U.S. Department of State and administered by the Institute of International Education (IIE). The Humphrey Program at American University Washington College of Law is part of a nationwide international exchange program, which includes 11 U.S. colleges and universities. The program builds connections between U.S. and global leaders to advance solutions to the world’s most pressing challenges — from combatting climate change and promoting human rights to expanding access to education and strengthening global health security. Rooted in leadership development, academic study, professional collaboration, and cultural exchange, the Humphrey Fellowship fosters mutual understanding and transformative impact. Learn more: www.humphreyfellowship.org/program-overview Essential Functions: Assist in planning and executing program events (virtual and in-person), including orientation, workshops, certificate ceremony, and cultural excursions. Provide administrative and logistical support for the Humphrey Fellowship Program including weekly Humphrey Seminar, panels, and other events as needed. Maintain and update the Humphrey Program website and social media accounts (Facebook, IG and LinkedIn) to ensure accuracy and accessibility of information. Offer comprehensive support to Humphrey Fellows, including assistance with class registration, and general program-related inquiries. Manage various administrative tasks, including making room reservations, and maintaining program documentation. Assist in preparing reports, communications, presentations, and correspondence for internal and external stakeholders, including program sponsors. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 20 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.40 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Currently enrolled in a graduate degree program. Demonstrated organizational and multitasking abilities, with a high level of professionalism. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Strong initiative and problem-solving skills with the ability to work independently and collaboratively. Preferred Education and Experience: Interest in international education, leadership development, or cross-cultural exchange preferred. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: 20 - 25 hours per week during the fall and spring semesters. 35 - 40 hours per week during the summer (June through mid-August). Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Spring-Valley-Campus-Washington-DC/Manager---Corporate-Card-and-Travel_R5127": {
      "title": "Manager - Corporate Card and Travel",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Accounts Payable Operations Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 02 (On Campus 2 days/Week) Union: Excluded Job Description: Summary: This position plays a vital role within the AU community and is responsible for all aspects of management, support, training, reporting, and compliance with University policies for the Corporate Card program, Travel Program and Rewards Card Program. This includes policy interpretation and guidance, regular transaction auditing and analysis and continuous support to University staff, faculty and students. Essential Functions: 1.) Corporate Card Program Management and Administration Oversee and administer all aspects of the University’s Corporate Card Program ensuring effective operations aligned with University policies and financial controls. Interpret, apply, and provide guidance on University policies and procedures related to card usage and expense management. Conduct reconciliations, audits and analysis of card transactions as requested to ensure compliance, identify discrepancies, mitigate risk, and recommend corrective actions as needed. Monitor program activity and generate reports as requested by leadership to review. Provide ongoing support to cardholders, approvers, and administrative staff, including card issuance, closure, reconciliation, spend limits, disputes, procedures regarding lost or stolen cards, troubleshooting issues, resolving discrepancies, etc. Deliver training, materials, and communications to educate users on proper card usage, policy updates, and best practices. Maintain accurate and up-to-date program documentation, including policies, procedures, and user guides. Identify opportunities to enhance program efficiency, strengthen controls, and improve the overall user experience. 2.) Travel Program Management and Administration Oversee and administer all aspects of the University’s Travel Program ensuring alignment with University’s goals, budgetary controls and policy requirement. Interpret, apply and provide guidance on University’s policies and procedures relating to travel. Manage relationship with travel management companies, airlines, hotels and expense tools, including negotiation, performance monitoring and service optimization. Provide ongoing support to travelers, approvers and administrative staff on their travel inquiries. Oversee travel booking and expense systems, ensuring proper configuration, functionality, and ways to integrate with financial systems. Deliver training, materials and communication programs to educate travelers and approvers on policies, tools, and best practices. Maintain accurate and up-to-date program documentation, including policies, procedures, and user guides. Identify opportunities to enhance program efficiency, strengthen controls, and improve traveler’s experience. 3.) Rewards Card Program Management and Administration Oversee and administer the University’s Rewards Card Program ensuring alignment with University’s policies and procedures. Provide ongoing support to administrators explaining the options, requirements and constraints of each option. Report program’s activity to Accounting, ensuring all reporting requirements are met. 4.) Other Duties as Assigned Additional duties as directed by the Director of Procurement and Associate VP, Financial Operations   Supervisory Responsibility: Currently supervising a part-time student with the potential for hiring a full-time assistant. Competencies: Championing Customer Needs. Collaborative Leadership. Prioritizing and Organizing. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Driving Continuous Improvement. Acting Strategically. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $104,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree. 2 - 4 years of relevant experience. Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree or equivalent. 3 - 5 years of relevant experience. Travel Required: Occasional travel is required. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/NSFP-Orientation-Chair--Student-_R5145": {
      "title": "NSFP Orientation Chair (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Logistics & Operations Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary: Orientation Chairs (OCs) are responsible for the efficient and effective implementation of all New Student and Family Programs projects and goals. To accomplish this, OCs will assist in welcoming and supporting all incoming first-year students, transfer students, and their families and supporters by leading a variety of efforts in preparation for and during New Student Orientation (NSO). This support continues through the All-American Welcome programming during the first weeks of the semester. Being an Orientation Chair requires a significant commitment and dedication to the work of supporting students and families in their transition to American University. There are six (6) vacant positions available. In addition to the general responsibilities of the OCs listed below, each Chair will also have the chance to focus on one of the areas of specialization below. Essential Functions: 1.) Orientation Planning Assist with the planning and execution of pre-arrival activities throughout the summer and in-person orientation activities and functions in August, including preparing materials, working with campus partners, compiling interest and assessment data, and supporting day-of logistics. Contribute to evaluation and assessment by tracking attendance, participating in staff debriefs, and leading team debriefs among Orientation Leaders. Other duties as assigned. 2.) O-Team Management Assist in the recruitment and interview process of the Orientation Leaders. Serve as team leader responsible for the oversight of a group of Orientation Leaders (OLs) as well as team motivator for the OLs during pre-arrival and in-person orientation activities and functions. Facilitate daily team meetings/debriefs for your group of OLs throughout New Student Orientation. Manage OTeam communications via email, social media, and other platforms. Manage OTeam staff bios and photos for website and other promotional outlets. Manage OTeam SharePoint and other digital and print materials. Other duties as assigned. 3.) Pre-Arrival Programming Support the Eagles Overnight Experience pilot program, a new-for-2027 program designed to minimize withdraw-after-deposit students by inviting students to participate in a two-day, overnight experience in June or July. Manage the New Student and Family Programs social media accounts. Contribute to and participate in the creation of digital content for new students and their families. Keep up to date with expectations of incoming students by reviewing the weekly New Eagle Newsletter that is sent to all new students throughout the summer. Other duties as assigned. 4.) In-person OL Training Facilitate various informational sessions during Orientation Leader training. Manage and confirm OL duties and assignments for the week. Serve as a point of contact and coordinator of daily orientation activities for your OLs. Other duties as assigned. 5.) New Student Orientation Serve as a guide and point of contact for all Orientation Leaders throughout the NSO program. Assist with day-of logistics, including but not limited to confirming NSO presenters arrive on time, ensuring all NSO spaces are unlocked and prepared in advance, line control and crowd management, and more. Foster a sense of belonging through active engagement in all in-person programming. Other duties as assigned. 6.) Merchandise and Marketing This Orientation Chair will work closely with the Assistant Director of Pre-Orientation and Operations, serving as the primary point of contact for all marketing, communications, and merchandise development for Pre-Orientation and NSO needs. This OC should be comfortable developing social media graphics, promotional videos, and office and program-specific swag materials. Scheduling and Logistics, Accessibility. With oversight from the Associate Director of Orientation, this Orientation Chair will focus on the physical logistics of the NSO schedule. This includes but is not limited to assigning breakout rooms for the OL Small Group sessions and the NSO Dialogue sessions, ensuring and documenting accessible routes to and from all on-campus locations, defining wayfinding locations for signage and NSO staff, and more. 7.)Team Camaraderie With oversight from the Associate Director of Orientation, this Orientation Chair will focus on efforts to increase camaraderie between members of the OTeam over the course of the summer, leading up to and continuing through OL Training and New Student Orientation. This may include but is not limited to social media campaigns, spirit weeks, planned meetups, and more. 8.) Training Content In conjunction with the Associate Director of Orientation, this Orientation Chair will focus on content creation as it relates to OL-led sessions during New Student Orientation. This includes but is not limited to updating the scripts and talking points for OL Small Groups, identifying ways to engage new students during planned or unplanned downtime, and more. This OC will also work closely with the Graduate Assistant for Orientation as the schedule and logistics for OL Training take shape. 9.) Pre-Orientation: AU Outdoors This OC will assist in the coordination and curriculum development of the AU Outdoors pre-orientation program. The OC will work with the Assistant Director of Pre-Orientation and Operations and should have a background facilitating outdoor excursions/adventures for incoming first-year students. Past experiences for this program include trips to Shenandoah Valley National Park and Terrapin Adventures. 10.) Pre-Orientation: Civics in Action This OC will assist in the coordination and curriculum development of the Civics in Action pre-orientation program. The OC will work with the Assistant Director of Pre-Orientation and Operations. The ideal candidate for the Civics in Action OC will have interest and/or involvement in public service, law, or leadership development, and interest in welcoming incoming first-year students. Past experiences for this program included alumni talks, presentations from DC public servants, leadership development workshops, and a tour of the US Capitol. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Part-time. 15 hours per week. Salary Range: $18.90 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Successfully served as part of a previous orientation team since the introduction of the new orientation model (Fall 2024 and onward). Enrolled as an undergraduate student at American University having completed at least 30 credits. Maintain a minimum cumulative grade point average of 3.0. Be in good judicial and academic standing with the university. Demonstrate a sincere interest in helping new students and their families feel welcomed and supported in their transition to American University. Be willing to support activities and programs related to diversity, inclusion, sexual consent, and community standards. Training and guidance provided, if necessary. Work well with others and communicate effectively. Demonstrate patience, flexibility, strong organizational skills, and a willingness to learn. Possess a strong sense of responsibility to manage multiple tasks with little supervision. Display public relation skills and maturity of judgment as an ambassador of AU. Must exhibit cultural competency and knowledge in topics of diversity, equity, and inclusion. Be willing to accept a challenge and step out of your comfort zone. Work both independently and within a team environment. Troubleshoot and solve problems effectively and efficiently. Work effectively with diverse populations. Learn and use new and challenging computer programs. Demonstrate excellent written, verbal, organizational, time management, and interpersonal communication skills. Relate well to students, families, AU staff, and the public. Demonstrate leadership, initiative, motivation, professionalism, ingenuity, attention to detail, understanding, compassion, desire to succeed, and strong work ethic. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: All Orientation Chairs must be available for all spring, summer, and fall dates. For a current list of these dates, please contact the Associate Director of Orientation. During the spring semester and the summer, it is possible for OCs to hold other on- or off-campus jobs, internships, etc. as long as they do not conflict with your ability to complete your OC responsibilities. You cannot hold any other positions, have other commitments, etc. from the beginning of OL Training through the end of New Student Orientation; this includes both on- and off-campus commitments. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Spring-Valley-Campus-Washington-DC/Director-of-Enrollment-Analytics-and-CRM_R4452": {
      "title": "Director of Enrollment Analytics and CRM",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Enrollment Data Analytics Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: Job Description: Summary: The Director of Enrollment Analytics and CRM serves as the functional lead for Salesforce CRM operations, undergraduate enrollment analytics, predictive modeling, and operational reporting. The role is responsible for ensuring that enrollment technologies, admissions workflows, and analytical tools effectively support student recruitment, application management, and enrollment goals. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President of Enrollment Analytics and Operations, the position is responsible for expanding the Office of Undergraduate Enrollment’s analytical capacity through sophisticated predictive modeling, pipeline analysis, scenario planning, and dashboard development. The role combines hands-on Salesforce administration, Workday Integration with advanced statistical analysis to improve recruitment outcomes, strengthen forecasting accuracy, support class shaping decisions, and enhance the efficiency and integrity of undergraduate enrollment operations. This position plays a central role in advancing predictive and prescriptive enrollment strategy by leading the development of enrollment models, funnel analyses, yield forecasts, melt projections, and scenario-based planning tools that inform recruitment planning, financial aid strategy, communications strategy, territory management, and leadership decision-making. The role also ensures that Salesforce CRM and Workday ERP are configured and maintained to support strong data quality, workflow automation, user adoption, and scalable enrollment operations. This position provides leadership in evaluating and implementing CRM enhancements, streamlining business processes, resolving operational system issues, and supporting the integrity and effectiveness of admissions technologies and enrollment data systems. The position works collaboratively with the offices of Undergraduate Admissions, Financial Aid, Information Technology, Institutional Research, Graduate Admissions, and external technology partners to support systems integration, prospect strategy, enrollment forecasting, and data-informed planning. Essential Functions: 1.) Enrollment Modeling, Predictive Analytics, and Forecasting Leadership In close collaboration with the AVP for Enrollment Analytics and Operations the Director of Enrollment Analytics and CRM will lead the design, development, validation, and refinement of enrollment models, predictive analytics, and forecasting tools that support undergraduate recruitment strategy and class planning for first-year and transfer populations. Analyzes prospect, inquiry, applicant, admit, deposit, financial aid, and enrollment data to identify patterns in student behavior, project enrollment outcomes, and improve strategic decision-making across the recruitment funnel. Develops statistical models and scenario analysis related to inquiry generation, application growth, conversion, class shaping, pricing strategy, yield, melt, and final enrollment outcomes. Produces market-responsive forecasts, year-over-year trend analyses, territory and segment performance analyses, and class-shaping models to support recruitment planning and executive decision-making. Builds analytical frameworks that assess the impact of student search strategy, outreach cadence, communications performance, campus engagement, and financial aid on pipeline movement and enrollment results. Translates complex quantitative findings into actionable recommendations, clear visualizations, and executive-ready decision-support materials for Enrollment leadership. Monitors model performance, evaluates forecast accuracy, and refines methodologies to improve predictive reliability and strategic usefulness over time. Ensures the integrity, consistency, and usability of data used for predictive modeling, forecasting, and enrollment planning. 2.) Salesforce CRM Administration and Enrollment Systems Leadership Serves as the primary point of contact, subject matter expert, and functional administrator for Salesforce CRM and related undergraduate enrollment technology platforms. Leads the administration, configuration, maintenance, enhancement, and optimization of Salesforce CRM to support first-year and transfer recruitment, admissions processing, communications workflows, and enrollment operations. Designs, documents, and maintains CRM business processes, workflow automations, user roles, permissions, validation rules, and operational procedures to improve efficiency, consistency, and compliance. Partners with Information Technology and external vendors to support Workday Student integrations, troubleshoot issues, resolve data exchange problems, and ensure reliable interoperability between enrollment systems. Coordinates system testing, quality assurance, release readiness, and implementation of CRM enhancements and related technology improvements. Monitors system performance, data quality, workflow execution, and user needs to identify opportunities for continuous improvement and operational streamlining. Develops training materials and delivers training for admissions and enrollment staff on Salesforce functionality, reporting tools, and operational best practices. Supports data governance, process documentation, and change management activities related to CRM administration and enrollment systems operations. 3.) Reporting, Dashboards, and Strategic Decision Support Designs, develops, and maintains standard and ad hoc reports, dashboards, scorecards, and data visualizations for the Office of Undergraduate Enrollment and campus stakeholders. Creates reporting solutions that support operational management, executive oversight, and strategic planning across prospect development, application processing, admissions, financial aid coordination, and enrollment performance. Produces recurring and ad hoc analyses related to funnel health, conversion trends, recruitment outcomes, territory performance, and enrollment projections. Responds to internal and external requests for admissions and enrollment data, providing interpretation and context for technical and non-technical audiences. Supports workflow improvement and operational effectiveness through process analysis, documentation, reporting enhancements, and improvements to data access and reporting practices. 4.) Cross-Functional Collaboration, Compliance, and Strategic Support Serves as a liaison to Information Technology, Institutional Research, Financial Aid, Graduate Admissions, and external technology partners to support system functionality, integrations, reporting alignment, and data exchange processes. Participates in strategic enrollment management initiatives, Salesforce enhancement planning, systems implementation efforts, and technology modernization projects supporting undergraduate enrollment goals. Ensures compliance with institutional policies, data governance standards, privacy expectations, and applicable regulations related to student data, system access, and information security. Contributes technical and analytical expertise to cross-functional planning discussions related to recruitment strategy, operational design, and enrollment performance improvement. Supervisory Responsibility: Yes, reporting directly to this position is a Data Analyst. Competencies: Prioritizing and Organizing. Developing Plans. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Acting Strategically. Collaborative Leadership. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Salary Range: $123,984.00 - $150,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree required in information systems, data analytics, computer science, statistics, mathematics, economics, business analytics, higher education administration, or a related field. 5 - 8 years of relevant experience. Minimum of three years of progressively responsible experience in enrollment analytics, predictive modeling, CRM administration, enrollment systems, institutional analysis, or enrollment management operations. Demonstrated experience developing enrollment forecasts, predictive models, scenario analyses, dashboards, and analytical reports to support strategic decision-making. Demonstrated experience administering Salesforce CRM or a comparable enterprise CRM platform, including configuration, workflow support, user management, and troubleshooting. Strong technical proficiency with CRM platforms, SQL, reporting and dashboard tools, data visualization platforms, spreadsheets, and statistical or analytical software applications. Strong analytical, organizational, project management, and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines in a complex operational environment. Ability to communicate technical concepts, analytical findings, and system requirements effectively to both technical and non-technical audiences. Preferred Education and Experience: Master’s degree in analytics, statistics, information systems, higher education, business analytics, economics, or a related field. 8 - 10 years of relevant experience. Salesforce Administrator certification or comparable CRM platform certification. Experience in undergraduate admissions, enrollment management, financial aid analysis, or higher education CRM administration. Experience with Workday Student integrations, higher education admissions technologies, and data governance practices. Experience using statistical methods and tools for predictive modeling, segmentation, scenario planning, funnel analysis, and enrollment forecasting. Familiarity with business process improvement, workflow automation, and change management practices in complex organizations. Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Front-Desk-Support--Student-_R5150": {
      "title": "Front Desk Support (Student)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nPart time\n\nWork Where You Learn: Build Experience, Grow Skills, and Contribute to Your University Community.   This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Most student positions at American University are eligible to use Federal Work Study (FWS) awards, with a few exceptions. FWS funds cannot be applied to janitorial, construction, partisan or sectarian positions. For information or questions regarding FWS funds, please contact American University's Office of Financial Aid.   Important guidance for current American University employees: American University current employees, including those employed in student positions, must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into your employee Workday account and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Applying outside of your employee Workday account may cause delays in a hire process. Department: Kogod School of Business Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Summary:    Career Development at the Kogod School of Business at American University supports undergraduate and graduate business students in their professional development. Front Desk Coordinators (FDCs) are student employees who serve as the first point of contact for all visitors to the office, including students, employers, alumni, faculty, and staff. FDCs provide essential administrative and customer service support in a fast-paced, student- and employer-facing environment.    Essential Functions:  Staff the front desk during business hours (9:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m., Monday–Friday) on a scheduled basis.  Serve as the first point of contact for the Kogod Career Development office—greeting guests, answering phones, and responding to or redirecting emails from the kogodcareers@american.edu account.  Support students to make and check-in for appointments for students with Career Development staff and Peer Consultants.  Provide support for and troubleshoot student and employer issues in Handshake, Kogod’s job and internship platform.  Support with the Kogod Talent Network system.  Monitor and assess the Document Review process in KTN (assess, request feedback, and approve undergraduate resumes based on criteria).  Create alumni accounts.  Monitor the Kogod Talent Network email inbox for inbound requests.  Support basic troubleshooting and answer basic questions about what the system does.  Review and approve content such as blog posts and videos for UConnect (the career website platform).  Assist with content creation, social media support, and presentation development.  Support general office operations and administrative projects such as data entry, filing, copying, and maintaining shared spaces.  Provide backup assistance for event and space setup, including flipping the lounge for events or meetings.    Position Type/Expected Hours of Work:   Part-time.  8 - 15 hours per week.    Salary Range:  $18.40 per hour.    Required Education and Experience:  Students pursuing an undergraduate degree.  Strong leadership and communication skills.  Impeccable organizational skills and attention to detail.  Flexibility, initiative, and ability to multitask and work both independently and as part of a team.  Maturity and an elevated level of professionalism, especially as it relates to building and maintaining relationships with prospective and visiting employers.  Proficiency with Microsoft Office Products.    Preferred Education and Experience:  Kogod undergraduate student.  Students available for both the fall and spring semesters preferred.    Additional Eligibility Qualifications:  1.) All Kogod Career Development Roles  All student employees will receive cross-training in front desk operations, event setup and support, and social media assistance. Flexibility to assist in these areas as needed is expected.  Must be willing to sign and adhere to a Confidentiality Agreement to ensure the privacy and security of student records and other sensitive information.  Ability and willingness to lift up to 25 lbs and assist with moving furniture and equipment during event setup and teardown.    2.) Benefits of Working for the Kogod Career Development Be at the heart of career support, recruiting action, and interact with students, staff, faculty and employers.  Develop valuable experience in working with time management and customer service.  Become a master of networking and build your professional network.   3.) Other Applicants are strongly encouraged to attach a resume and cover letter to their applications. Other Details This position is available only to enrolled American University students. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Visit https://www.american.edu/hr/ for additional information about American University employment and benefits.   Current American University Employees American University current employees (including those employed in student positions) must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings.   Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu .   American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Tenley-Campus-Washington-DC/Practitioner-In-Residence_R5134": {
      "title": "Practitioner-In-Residence",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Washington College of Law Time Type: Full time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: American University Washington College of Law is seeking applications for a Practitioner-in-Residence for AY 2026-2027 in the Janet R. Spragens Federal Tax Clinic.  This is a term faculty appointment, with reappointment, starting November 23, 2026, through June 30, 2027. American University’s in-house, “live-client” Clinical Program, comprising eleven (11) in-house clinics and serving nearly 200 students per year, is respected for its leadership in scholarship, development of clinical methodology, contributions to increasing access to justice for under-served clients and breadth of offerings. The Tax Clinic provides legal representation, education, and advocacy for low-income individuals with disputes against the Internal Revenue Service. Cases include audits, administrative appeals, refund claims, collection disputes, and other matters before the IRS or before a federal court. The Clinic also provides community education and engages in systemic advocacy efforts. The overarching goals are to protect and advance the legal rights of low-income individuals in the tax system, and to improve the equity of the tax system. The Practitioner-in-Residence Program, created in 1998, is a program designed to train lawyers or entry-level clinicians interested in becoming clinical teachers in the practice and theory of clinical legal education.  Many graduates of the Practitioner-in-Residence program have gone on to tenure-track or other long-term teaching positions at other law schools. Practitioners supervise student casework, co-teach weekly clinic seminars and case rounds, and engage in course planning and preparation with the faculty member who directs the clinic that they teach in. They also teach a course outside of the clinical curriculum after their first year of teaching.  The Practitioner-in-Residence Program provides training in clinical theory and methodology and assists Practitioners in the development of their scholarship. Minimum qualifications include a JD degree, outstanding academic record, three years’ experience as a lawyer (or equivalent work experience) and membership in a state bar. The annualized salary for the position is $90,000. Applications that include a curriculum vitae and cover letter should be submitted online via Workday. Please contact Erica Devine, Faculty Affairs Manager, at edevine@american.edu if you have any general questions regarding the application process and contact Professor Christine Speidel, Director of the Janet R. Spragens Federal Tax Clinic, at cspeidel@wcl.american.edu , for any other questions about the position. The position will remain open until filled. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Executive-Director--Women---Politics-Institute_R5153": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Women & Politics Institute",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: School of Public Affairs Time Type: Full time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: American University’s School of Public Affairs’ Women & Politics Institute seeks an experienced and entrepreneurial leader, who has a demonstrated record advancing the empowerment and representation of women, to serve as Executive Director of the Women & Politics Institute. The appointment is a non-tenure track term faculty position at the rank of Senior Professorial Lecturer in the Department of Government and will commence on January 15, 2027, through August 31, 2028. The Women & Politics Institute provides academic and practical training that encourages involvement in the political process and facilitates research by faculty and students that enhances our understanding of the challenges women face in the political arena. The Institute offers graduate and undergraduate certificates in Women, Policy, and Political Leadership, sponsors a weekly webinar series, as well as a training program for all individuals seeking to explore careers in the political arena. The Executive Director will devise, implement, and manage the Institute’s strategic plan and fundraising strategy. The Executive Director will also oversee the Institute’s personnel, operations, budget, and external communications. At a minimum, a master’s degree, or equivalent professional experience, is preferred. We are especially interested in applicants who have had substantial high-level management or policy experience in government, international organizations, not-for-profits, or business. The candidate should be able to demonstrate a talent for and interest in teaching and mentoring students. We seek practitioners who can help us develop new educational programs, facilitate new partnerships, and raise the visibility of the Institute and School. This position will require some weekend and evening hours. In addition to serving as the executive of the Institute, the incumbent will be expected to teach 2 courses (total 6 credits) each academic year, in consultation with the Department of Government.   Salary and benefits are competitive, with an expected annual salary range of $120,000-$150,000. Review of applications will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled, subject to ongoing budgetary approval. The position begins on January 15, 2027. Please submit applications via Workday and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and the names of three references. Please contact Joanne Weisman, Department of Government Administrative Coordinator, at jwiesman@american.edu if you have any questions. American University is a private institution within easy reach of the many centers of government, business, research, and the arts located within the nation’s capital. For more information about American University, visit www.american.edu. The Department of Government in the School of Public Affairs offers both undergraduate and graduate degree programs. Learn more about SPA at https://www.american.edu/spa/ and about the department at https://www.american.edu/spa/gov/. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Coordinator--Student-Engagement-and-Traditions_R5136-1": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Student Engagement and Traditions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Program Initiatives Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Student Engagement and Traditions Coordinator supports the development and execution of campus-wide engagement initiatives that foster student thriving, community connection, and active involvement at American University. This position plays a key role in advising major student programming boards and contributes to the planning and delivery of a wide range of events such as Welcome Weeks, late-night programs, recurring and weekly student engagement programs such as Red Tuesdays, Thank Clawed It’s Wednesday, Thursday Night Live, Friday Night Films and Saturday Night Socials university festivals, class-year programs, and signature traditions. The Coordinator is responsible for event logistics, budget management, adherence to university policies, and supervision of graduate and undergraduate student staff. Success in this role requires strong project management skills, a deep commitment to student development, and the flexibility to work evenings and weekends as needed. Through intentional programming and student support, this position helps create a vibrant, inclusive campus culture that enhances the overall student experience, builds school spirit and promotes community building. Essential Functions: 1.) Student Advising and Training Meet regularly with student leadership cohorts within the assigned functional areas to provide proactive feedback, individualized support, and strategic guidance that promotes personal development, leadership growth, and organizational success. This position serves as the primary advisor for Students for Change, Spirit and Traditions Board, and as the co-advisor for the Student Activity Council, offering ongoing mentorship and high-level event advising, and provides support to other programming boards as needed. In partnership with the Associate Director for Student Programs and Signature Events, the Coordinator will support the planning and implementation of Student Activity Council retreats, trainings and workshops, including collaborations with campus partners. Through this work, the Coordinator fosters a student-centered approach to programming that enhances campus life and encourages collaboration, innovation, and school pride. In coordination with the Associate Director, this role will support the larger student organization community and university as a whole, with the planning and execution of complex, large-scale events and signature campus-wide traditions.   2.) Student Engagement and Campus Programming This position is responsible for planning and implementing a broad range of programs that enhance the student experience and contribute to a vibrant campus life. This role involves working collaboratively with internal and external stakeholders to ensure successful event execution, while continuously refining programming based on student feedback and assessment data. In partnership with the broader Student Engagement team, the Coordinator will support and channel student advocacy, energy, and creativity into the development of a dynamic and inclusive campus experience. This includes establishing new campus traditions with students that foster community and belonging among students, faculty, and staff, as well as designing, implementing, sustaining, and enhancing existing signature events. The Coordinator will also help shape and implement a strategy to increase AU spirit and pride, grounded in the university’s history and culture. Key responsibilities include supporting the planning and execution of Welcome Weeks and a portfolio of recurring student engagement programs, including Red Tuesdays, Thank Clawed It’s Wednesday, Thursday Night Live, and Eagles at Night. The Coordinator will co-chair the Thank Clawed It's Wednesday and Eagles at Night committees, working collaboratively with campus partners to implement these recurring programs. Eagles at Night includes Friday Night Films and Saturday Night Socials, with the Coordinator providing regular staffing support for Friday Night Films in partnership with the Student Engagement team. The Coordinator will also develop cohort and class-based programming for sophomore, transfer, and junior students, with a focus on fostering engagement, connection, and retention.    3.) Administration and Budget Management Manage budget and oversee funding process for student cohort groups and programming within functional area. Ensure fiscal responsibility, effective planning, effective use of resources, and compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and University policies and procedures. Create end-of-year reports for assigned functional area. Represent department on university and division-wide committees, including but not limited to, the University Communications Collaborative, and represent the department on All-American Welcome planning committees. Oversee and manage contracting processes for assigned programs and student organizations in accordance with University policies and procedures. Develop and implement systems for assessing student engagement, satisfaction, and feedback related to program attendance and participation, to inform programmatic improvements and demonstrate the impact of engagement. Coordinate program storage, supplies, and resource management. Supervises assigned graduate and undergraduate staff.   4.) Marketing, Communications, and Event Production This position oversees the department’s digital presence, including the maintenance of the departmental website, newsletters, and related student organization pages. The role manages and sustains a robust and creative social media presence across platforms, with primary responsibility for the CSI Instagram account, social media content calendar, photography, video content, graphic design, and marketing campaigns, ensuring consistent engagement with the campus community for department-led programs and student engagement initiatives. The Coordinator will also coordinate departmental submissions to Student Affairs newsletters and other university-wide communication channels. Serve as the department’s primary point for CSI event pages on the student organization management platform, maintaining event information, records, forms and assessment data in collaboration with the Associate Director. The Coordinator will provide marketing and creative support for student engagement initiatives, including developing marketing campaigns, supporting program branding and providing guidance on promotional strategies and materials. Demonstrating a strong passion for creativity and student outreach, the position collaborates closely with the Student Affairs marketing team and the University Communications & Marketing office on content strategy, social media planning and posting, and day-of content coverage for departmental events and student engagement initiatives. In addition, the position utilizes strong project management and organizational skills to lead the execution of assigned events, overseeing all production elements including audiovisual requirements, space setup, facilities coordination, staging, and other logistical needs to ensure successful event delivery Support major division and university-wide programs, including but not limited to, Commencement, Family Weekend, Summer Send-Offs, Move-In, New Student Orientation, Family and Supporters Orientation, Student Achievement Awards, and Eagles at Night. As a member of the Division of Student Affairs, this position may advise recognized student organizations as needed. Supervisory Responsibility: Supervise student staff and graduate assistants. Competencies: Serving Customers. Displaying Creativity. Prioritizing and Organizing. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Supporting Coworkers. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours week. Staff will work on an adjusted work schedule as needed, including late nights and weekends to support student programs. Salary Range: $50,000.00 - $55,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree. 1 - 3 years of relevant experience.   Preferred Education and Experience:  Master's degree.  2 - 4 years of relevant experience.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications:   Inclusive Excellence: An ability to welcome, value, and affirm individuals of all identities and experiences, and a commitment to ensure they are understood, appreciated, and fully included in the university community. Discretion, Tact and Diplomacy: Ability to exercise discretion, tact and diplomacy while interacting with students, faculty, university staff, management, internship employers and other stakeholders on confidential and/or sensitive matters. Advanced Communication: Ability to express oneself clearly and concisely, using language with precision; constructing logical arguments; accurate note taking, editing and summarizing; and writing reports or correspondence free of spelling and grammatical errors. Ability to engage in active and empathetic listening and communicate with a service minded orientation.    Thoroughness in Reviewing Work: Ability to review one’s own and others work and information to ensure completeness and accuracy; carefully prepare for meetings and presentations; organize information or materials for others; follow up with others to ensure that agreements and commitments have been fulfilled; and set up procedures to ensure high quality of work. Student Services Skills: Ability to serve as bridge to services, intervene and/or advocate on students’ behalf with offices across campus to support needs, drive engagement and empower students to achieve their goals. Diagnostic Information Gathering: Ability to seek out relevant people to obtain information; probe skillfully to get at the facts when others are reluctant to disclose the full details; ask clarifying questions; and gain a complete and accurate picture by checking multiple sources. Standard Office Software: Skill using email, word processing, spreadsheet/ data analysis and presentation development software applications such as Microsoft Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint while preparing briefings, reports, and presentations.   Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Merit-Awards-Program-Coordinator_R5139": {
      "title": "Merit Awards Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Merit Awards Associate Director 2 Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Non-Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division This position is part of a collective bargaining unit represented by SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. That means the terms and conditions of your employment are governed by university policy (including the Staff Personnel Policies Manual) and any collective bargaining agreement entered into between American University and SEIU Local 500 - Provost & Enrollment Division. Job Description: Summary: The Office of Merit Awards oversees the mentoring of nationally competitive, merit-based scholarships that fund study abroad, graduate tuition, and summer research.  The Merit Awards Program Coordinator provides essential support to the Office to further the university’s strategic goal pertaining to academic excellence.  Primary responsibilities are project management and customer service, including: organizing scholarship information sessions and workshops; supporting the Associate  Director in the organization of major annual events; compiling data for the office and drafting the annual report; recruiting the university’s highest performing undergraduate and graduate students; coordinating faculty interview panels, maintaining an award database; helping to publicize award recipients; updating the website, and managing the office’s social media accounts. Assists the office’s Associate Director with the Early Identification Program (EIP) for outstanding sophomores.  Works with students, faculty and staff mentors, and national scholarship foundation representatives.    He/she supervises and trains a part-time program assistant and up to two Federal Work Study (FWS) students. The Program Coordinator is also responsible for overseeing front-line service in the form of scheduling appointments and interacting with students, parents, and other clients, with support from 1-2 part-time program assistants/FWS students.    This is a union-eligible position.    Essential Functions: 1.) Project Management Organizes student scholarship information sessions. Arranges faculty committees and interview panels on behalf of national finalists and applicants for multiple scholarships, such as the Truman, Rhodes, UK and Boren scholarships. Supports the Senior Director in organizing meetings with the CAS Merit Awards STEM Committee. Assists the office’s Associate Director with organizing and running large events, including the Early Identification Program Reception and end-of-year breakfast. Arranges dozens of small-group workshops and panel presentations each year.    2.) Front-Desk Service, Supervision, and Scheduling Recruits, trains, and supervises two student employees including the OMA program assistant responsible for delivering front line customer service. Manages and oversees the front desk and inquiries directed to the office’s general email account (meritawards@american.edu). Works with the Career Center’s Customer Service and Resource Coordinator to ensure seamless customer service between the Office of Merit Awards and Career Center offices.   3.) Marketing and Publicity  Prepares and maintains the Office’s primary calendar. Anticipates and publicizes campus and national competition deadlines, advertises workshops and EIP events, oversees EIP newsletter, and reserves spaces for events (in person and virtual). Manages the office's social media presence to promote scholarship opportunities, program deadlines, and student success stories, and to support broader campus visibility for the office. Collaborates with the Career Center and AU Abroad. 4.) Technology and Reporting In consultation with the Career Center’s Systems Coordinator, implement technical systems, processes and equipment that will support the mission of the office. Develops year end annual report for the Provost and senior leadership, Collects and highlights recipient biographies to illustrate in the end of year report.   5.) General Administration and Other Duties as Assigned On behalf of Merit Awards staff, and in coordination with the Career Center staff, handles telecommunications requests, equipment repairs and supply purchasing. Takes part in Career Center-wide activities as needed. May provide backup support to the Career Center front desk at busy times. Supports the overall administrative functions of the office. Supervisory Responsibility: Supervises a part-time program assistant and two student employees. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Hybrid 1 work modality (on campus 3 - 4 days per week). Salary Range: $30.00 - $33.00 per hour. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 1 - 3 years of relevant experience.   Preferred Education and Experience: 2 - 4 years of relevant experience. Experience with Outlook, Qualtrics and learning management systems such as Canvas. Experience in a higher education environment. Experience with social media management. Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Exceptionally strong organizational, writing and editing skills. Ability to interact diplomatically with faculty members and scholarship foundation representatives. Understanding of and desire to serve academically high-achieving students. Proven track record of reliability and superior attention to detail. Solid computer skills, including proficiency with Microsoft Word and Excel.    Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Program-Manager--Mentoring-Programs_R5138": {
      "title": "Program Manager, Mentoring Programs",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: Employer Relations Time Type: Full time Job Type: Regular FLSA Status: Exempt Work Modality: Hybrid 01 (On Campus 3-4 Days/Week) Union: Job Description: Summary: The Program Manager, Mentoring Programs provides strategic leadership, program oversight, and cross-campus coordination for American University’s university-wide alumni mentoring initiative. This role leads the implementation and continuous improvement of a comprehensive mentoring ecosystem that ensures all sophomores and transfer students have the opportunity to engage meaningfully with alumni mentors and build durable professional networks.   The Program Manager collaborates closely with University Advancement, academic departments, student affairs units, and employer engagement teams to develop a sustainable, high-impact mentoring program that supports student success, alumni engagement, and institutional goals.   This role also oversees program assessment, operational strategy, and professional staff supervision.   Essential Functions: 1.) Program Strategy & Leadership Provide strategic direction and long-term planning for the alumni mentoring program. Design scalable mentoring models, participation structures, policies, and workflows to support universal student access. Develop frameworks for mentor recruitment, onboarding, engagement, training, and recognition. Lead continuous improvement using assessment data, participation metrics, and stakeholder feedback. Serve as a strategic advisor to senior leadership on alumni engagement and professional networking initiatives.   2.) Cross-Campus & Alumni Partnerships Build and maintain close partnerships with University Advancement and Alumni Relations to align outreach, engagement strategies, and stewardship. Collaborate with academic units, faculty, and student success offices to embed mentoring into curricular and co-curricular pathways. Partner with employer engagement and experiential learning teams to align mentoring with internships, career readiness, and professional development. Represent the mentoring initiative in campus-wide planning and advisory groups.   3.) Program Oversight & Operations Management Oversee mentoring program operations, including mentor recruitment, student onboarding, matching processes, engagement models, and compliance. Ensure inclusive and equitable access to mentoring for all student populations. Develop policies, training materials, toolkits, and operational procedures. Oversee platform utilization and CRM systems used for mentoring engagement. Serve as the primary liaison to the mentoring platform vendor, holding regular meetings to oversee system updates, monitor utilization, address issues, and ensure the platform supports program goals and user needs.   4.) Supervision & Team Leadership Supervise the Program Coordinator and any graduate assistants or student staff. Provide coaching, professional development, and performance management. Establish workflows, performance goals, and accountability structures.   5.) Assessment, Data & Reporting Develop program metrics and dashboards to track mentor and student participation, engagement quality, satisfaction, retention, and outcomes. Prepare reports and presentations for senior leadership, advancement leadership, and strategic planning processes. Use data to inform program improvements and demonstrate institutional impact.   6.) Other Duties as Assigned Supervisory Responsibility: This role supervises the Program Coordinator, Mentoring Program and may supervise student assistants. Competencies: Prioritizing and Organizing. Managing Performance. Evaluating and Implementing Ideas. Making Accurate Judgments and Decisions. Collaborative Leadership. Acquiring and Analyzing Information. Building and Supporting Teams. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work: Full-time. 35 hours per week. Hybrid 1 work modality (3 - 4 days on campus). Salary Range: $85,000.00 - $90,000.00 annually. Required Education and Experience: Bachelor's degree or equivalent. 4 - 6 years of relevant experience.   Preferred Education and Experience: Master's degree or equivalent. 5 - 8 years of relevant experience.   Travel Required: Some local and possibly domestic travel.   Additional Eligibility Qualifications: Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive excellence and student success. Excellent written, verbal, and presentation skills. Strong organizational and project management skills. Ability to analyze data and translate insights into program improvements. Comfort working in highly collaborative environments.   Other Duties: Please note this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities and activities may change at any time with or without notice. Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    },
    "https://american.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/AU/job/Main-Campus-Washington-DC/Adjunct-Faculty---CAS-Department-of-World-Languages-and-Cultures_R962": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - CAS Department of World Languages and Cultures (Open Continuous)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nPart time\n\nAmerican University is a student-centered research institution located in Washington, DC, with highly-ranked schools and colleges, internationally-renowned faculty, and a reputation for creating meaningful change in the world. Learn more about American University Department: College of Arts & Sciences Time Type: Part time FLSA Status: Exempt Job Description: This is an open continuous vacancy announcement that will be used to fill adjunct vacancies as they occur. Until you hear from a representative of the Department of World Languages and Cultures or the Office of HR Faculty, please continue to also apply directly to other positions posted on AU's website. The Department of World Languages and Cultures in the College of Arts and Sciences at American University invites applications for a number of adjunct faculty positions for upcoming Academic Years. Rank will depend on an applicant’s highest degree earned in World Languages and Cultures or related fields. The applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The Department of World Languages and Cultures offers both undergraduate and graduate degree programs.  The Department is organized under the College of Arts and Sciences, which is where scholarship in the Arts, Humanities, and Sciences is pursued.  Learn more about the College of Arts and Sciences at https://www.american.edu/cas/ and the Department of World Languages and Cultures at https://www.american.edu/cas/wlc/ . The Department of World Languages and Cultures in the College of Arts and Sciences has the following adjunct salary ranges: Candidates Without a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $2,865 3-4 Credit Course: $4,725 5-6 Credit Course: $4,938 Candidates With a Terminal Degree In Course Subject Area 1-2 Credit Course: $3,414 3-4 Credit Course: $5,400 5-6 Credit Course: $5,548 Benefits AU offers a competitive benefits package.  Click here to learn about American University's unique benefit options . Other Details Hiring offers for this position are contingent on successful completion of a background check. Employees in staff positions at American University must deliver their services to the university from either the District of Columbia, Maryland, or Virginia, or perform work on-site at the university. Please note this job announcement is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time with or without notice. American University is an E-Verify employer. Current American University Employees American University current employees must apply through their employee Workday account. If you are a current employee at American University, please log into Workday and select the Find Jobs report which will take you to our internal career listings. Contact Us For more information or assistance with the American University careers site, email  theworkline@american.edu . American University is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution that operates in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. The university does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, religion, sex (including pregnancy), age, sexual orientation, disability, marital status, personal appearance, gender identity and expression, family responsibilities, political affiliation, source of income, veteran status, an individual’s genetic information or any other bases under federal or local laws (collectively \"Protected Bases\") in its programs and activities."
    }
